65	\title{\bfseries Upending Quantum Mechanics\\[0.3em]
66	\large Tensor Filtration and the Paraconsistent Ambient}
67	\author{C. Lando Mills\\ \small Trabuco Canyon, California}
68	\date{3 September 2026}
69	
70	\begin{document}
71	\maketitle
72	
73	\begin{abstract}
74	\noindent
75	Quantum mechanics is the classical-logical image of a paraconsistent four-valued logic. On this account, the ambient structure is the paraconsistent trilattice of sixteen values. The consistent classical world is a restriction of that structure, reached by two retractions that discard the states carrying contradiction and incompleteness.
76	
77	This paper develops the mechanics of that reading and grounds it in a complete worked instance. We first build the carrier explicitly, including its three orders, its negation, and its consequence relation. We then prove that the classical Boolean values form a proper part of the carrier, reached by two distinct adjunctions, ex falso and ex quodlibet, and that the paraconsistent value cannot be expressed inside either classical image.
78	
79	The operation at the center of the account is tensor filtration. We realize it as an explicit ladder of raising, lowering, and diagonal operators on the weight sectors of a many-coordinate register, and show that measurement is filtration through this ladder rather than collapse.
80	
81	We then give a full worked instance in which the classical image of a paraconsistent object is computed and measured. Optimal Polynomial Intersection has a quantum algorithm, Decoded Quantum Interferometry, whose expected performance is governed by the largest eigenvalue of a finite tridiagonal matrix. We show that this matrix is the ladder of the split, fuse, and evaluate operations on a paraconsistent register, and that the algorithm's expectation value is that ladder read through the coreflector. Across four scales, we compute both the finite operator-valued value and its asymptotic scalar limit, and measure the richer finite description collapsing onto the poorer limit in exactly the direction the coreflector moves.
82	\end{abstract}
83	
84	\tableofcontents
85	
86	\section{Introduction}
87	
88	The physics of the last century is usually told as a story of a classical world disturbed. There is a definite world of things with definite properties, and quantum mechanics is the strange correction that appears on close inspection: particles in two places, cats alive and dead, measurements that create the reality they record. The classical world is the ground and the quantum is the anomaly laid over it.
89	
90	This program inverts that story and shows the inversion is forced. The ground is not classical. The ambient logic of the world is paraconsistent, a four-valued logic in which a proposition can carry support for its truth and support for its falsity at once without the whole system exploding into triviality. The consistent two-valued world is not the floor beneath the quantum; it is a restriction cut out of the paraconsistent ambient after the fact, a proper part reachable from the whole by a retraction that throws information away. Quantum mechanics is the malformed image of the ambient itself, the lifeless classical slice we are afforded when we finish tossing it through the shredder. The world does not appear classical at any scale; the classical description is a bookkeeping laid over the ambient that declines to look at the structure constituting it.
91	
92	One might object that this is merely a relabeling, that the physics remains untouched. But the account makes a claim that can be false: that measurement is reversible filtration through a specific ladder, and that a specific quantum algorithm's matrix is that ladder read through a specific retraction. The theorem proved later identifies the entries term for term, and the convergence measured across four scales shows the retraction's loss as a number that moves in the predicted direction. A relabeling would predict nothing; this predicts the convergence and the direction, and the ladder mechanics are derived rather than named.
93	
94	Another objection concerns the dimensionful constants of physics. Where do they come from if the ambient is logic? They do not come from the logic, and this paper does not claim they do. The action quantum, the angle-to-length scale, and the dimensionful couplings enter from outside exactly where $\pi$ enters the winding, as the readout units. The logic fixes the dimensionless structure: the quadratic Born form, the Tsirelson bound, the tower of ranks. It hands the units to a measurement.
95	
96	Why paraconsistent logic rather than any many-valued logic? Two features are required together and only the paraconsistent carrier supplies both: a value fixed by negation, to carry the persistent correlation and the superposition, and a consequence relation that does not explode on that value, to keep the whole from collapsing to triviality. A many-valued logic without a negation-stable value cannot hold a superposition; one that explodes on it cannot survive the contradiction. The held value $B$ is both, and it is the one value the classical image cannot name.
97	
98	The world plainly looks classical at ordinary scale, one might insist. But it does not. A rock is held together by the exclusion of paired occupations across every orbital, the held value $B$ enforced everywhere at once; its solidity, its color, its refusal to collapse are that exclusion, not any one-sided fact. A genuinely classical object, one whose every proposition were settled true or false with no held part, would have no exclusion to give it volume and would not cohere. The classical description is a coarsening laid over the ambient that declines to read the structure holding the object up. What looks classical is the constructive shadow, the informational layer read as empty, and the shadow is never the ground.
99	
100	This paper carries two things the setting of the direction did not. First, it develops the mechanics of the operation at the center of the account. Measurement is not a collapse; it is a tensor filtration, a reversible selection of one factor of a tensor while the others remain. We make the filtration concrete as an explicit ladder of operators on the weight sectors of a many-coordinate register: a raising operator that opens a value into more support, its adjoint the lowering operator, and a diagonal operator that reads support already present. The three are the split, the fuse, and the evaluate operations of the grammar, realized as linear operators, and together they are a tridiagonal matrix whose structure is the whole content of the filtration.
101	
102	Second, it folds in a complete worked instance in which the classical image of a paraconsistent object is computed and measured. The problem is Optimal Polynomial Intersection, a hard combinatorial optimization over a finite field. Its state-of-the-art quantum algorithm, Decoded Quantum Interferometry, has an expected performance governed by the largest eigenvalue of a finite tridiagonal matrix. We show that this matrix is the ladder of the split, fuse, and evaluate operations on the register, and that the algorithm's expectation value is that ladder read through the coreflector. We compute the finite operator-valued value and its asymptotic scalar limit and measure the one collapsing onto the other across four scales, the richer description giving way to the poorer in the direction the coreflector moves.
103	
104	The spine of this paper is the filtration mechanics and the worked convergence, and everything is proved or computed on the page.
105	
106	\section{Background}
107	
108	The four-valued logic at the center of this account was not built for physics. It came from the question of how a computer should reason from a database that may hold, at once, a report that something is so and a report that it is not so. Belnap's answer was to give a proposition four possible epistemic states rather than two: told only that it is true, told only that it is false, told nothing, and told both. The two middle-breaking states, told nothing and told both, are the ones a two-valued logic cannot represent, and Belnap's point was that a reasoner facing real sources must represent them or fail. Dunn gave the matching semantics, reading each value as a pair of independent bits, support for truth and support for falsity, so the four values are the four subsets of a two-element set and the logic is the logic of that lattice. This is First-Degree Entailment, the fragment of relevance logic whose consequence relation keeps a contradiction from entailing everything.
109	
110	Ginsberg placed the structure in a wider setting with the bilattices, sets carrying two orders at once, one of information and one of truth, and Fitting developed their theory and their use in logic programming. Priest, from the direction of dialetheism, argued that some contradictions are true and that a logic must be paraconsistent to hold them without collapse, and the four-valued logic is the smallest such: its value told-both is a fixed point of negation that does not explode. Shramko, Dunn and Takenaka extended the bilattice to a trilattice by adding a second, informational layer of acceptability, giving sixteen values and three orders, information, truth, and constructivity, and it is their trilattice that this paper takes as the carrier. The lineage is a logic of sources and databases, and the claim of this program is that the same structure is the ambient logic of the physical world, with quantum mechanics its classical image.
111	
112	\section{The four-valued carrier}
113	
114	\subsection{Two layers, four base values, sixteen states}
115	
116	A four-valued logic asks a different question from a two-valued logic. Instead of asking only whether a proposition is true or false, it asks what information is present about that proposition. Support for truth and support for falsity are tracked independently.
117	
118	Two independent bits make up the constructive layer. The mark $T$ means that support for truth is present, while the mark $F$ means that support for falsity is present. If neither mark is present, the result is the undetermined value $N$. If both are present, the result is the overdetermined value $B$. These four values, $\{N,T,F,B\}$, are the Belnap four.
119	
120	The trilattice of Shramko, Dunn and Takenaka~\cite{sdt} adds a second, informational layer. This layer contains an acceptable value $t$ and a rejectable value $f$. They carry weight without settling truth. A value of the full carrier can contain any subset of the four base marks $\{T,F,t,f\}$, giving sixteen possible values in total.
121	
122	\begin{definition}[The carrier]
123	Let $\mathbf{16} = \mathcal{P}(\{T,F,t,f\})$, equivalently $\{0,1\}^4$, with bottom $N = \{\}$ and top $A = \{T,F,t,f\}$. The constructive layer of a value $x$ is $x \cap \{T,F\}$ and its informational layer is $x \cap \{t,f\}$.
124	\end{definition}
125	
126	The Belnap four consists of the values whose informational layer is empty: $N$, $T=\{T\}$, $F=\{F\}$, and $B=\{T,F\}$. Among these, the two one-sided values $T$ and $F$ are the classical values.
127	
128	\begin{center}
129	\begin{tabular}{l l c l l c}
130	\toprule
131	value & marks & info & value & marks & info \\
132	\midrule
133	$N$    & $\{\}$      & 0 & $tf$   & $\{t,f\}$     & 2 \\
134	$T$    & $\{T\}$     & 1 & $Ttf$  & $\{T,t,f\}$   & 3 \\
