301	
302	\begin{definition}[Boolean core and the two collapses]
303	Let $\mathbf{B}=\{F,T\}$ with inherited truth order and negation, the two-element Boolean algebra, exactly the Belnap values with $x\vee_{\mathrm t}\neg x = T$. Let the ex falso map $c$ and ex quodlibet map $r$ send $\{N,T,F,B\}$ to $\mathbf{B}$ by $c(T)=T,c(F)=F,c(N)=F,c(B)=F$ and $r(T)=T,r(F)=F,r(N)=T,r(B)=T$, and let $\iota$ be the inclusion.
304	\end{definition}
305	
306	\begin{theorem}[Two distinct adjunctions]\label{thm:two}
307	$c$ is monotone, fixes $\mathbf{B}$, and is right adjoint to $\iota$ in the truth order, $\iota(x)\leqt v \iff x\leqt c(v)$. $r$ is monotone, fixes $\mathbf{B}$, and is left adjoint, $r(v)\leqt x \iff v\leqt\iota(x)$. They differ on the nonclassical values, $r(N)=r(B)=T\neq F=c(N)=c(B)$, and agree on $\mathbf{B}$.
308	\end{theorem}
309	
310	\begin{proof}
311	The truth-order covers are $F\leqt N$, $F\leqt B$, $N\leqt T$, $B\leqt T$. Under $c$ they map to $F\leqt F,F\leqt F,F\leqt T,F\leqt T$, all true, so $c$ is monotone. The adjunction, two cases: for $x=F$ both $x\leqt c(v)$ and $\iota(x)\leqt v$ hold for all $v$ since $F$ is the bottom; for $x=T$, $x\leqt c(v)$ forces $c(v)=T$, holding exactly at $v=T$, and $\iota(T)\leqt v$ forces $v=T$. Under $r$ the covers map to $F\leqt T,F\leqt T,T\leqt T,T\leqt T$, all true; for $x=T$ both sides hold for all $v$, and for $x=F$, $r(v)\leqt F$ forces $r(v)=F$ at exactly $v=F$, while $v\leqt\iota(F)=F$ forces $v=F$. Distinctness is direct.
312	\end{proof}
313	
314	\begin{proposition}[Neither is an information-order adjunction]\label{prop:noinfo}
315	In the information order, where $N$ is the bottom and $B$ the top, neither $c$ nor $r$ is adjoint to $\iota$.
316	\end{proposition}
317	
318	\begin{proof}
319	Were $r$ left adjoint in $\leqi$, then $v=B$, $x=T$ would need $r(B)\leqi T \iff B\leqi\iota(T)=T$; the right side is false ($B$ is the top), the left side $T\leqi T$ true. Were $c$ right adjoint, $v=N$, $x=F$ would need $\iota(F)=F\leqi N \iff F\leqi c(N)=F$; the left side is false ($N$ is the bottom), the right true.
320	\end{proof}
321	
322	\begin{theorem}[The collapse is exactly lossy]\label{thm:lossy}
323	Let $\mathrm{inc}$ send every Belnap value to $B$. It is idempotent with unique fixed point $B$, and there is no map $\varphi:\mathbf{B}\to\mathbf{B}$ with $\iota\circ\varphi = \mathrm{inc}\circ\iota$: the value $B$ is inexpressible inside either classical image.
324	\end{theorem}
325	
326	\begin{proof}
327	$\mathrm{inc}(\mathrm{inc}(x))=\mathrm{inc}(B)=B=\mathrm{inc}(x)$, and a fixed point solves $x=B$. If $\varphi$ existed, $\iota(\varphi(x))=\mathrm{inc}(\iota(x))=B$ for classical $x$; but $\iota$ has image $\{F,T\}$, which excludes $B$, so no value of $\varphi(x)$ maps to $B$.
328	\end{proof}
329	
330	Each retraction is a total map onto the Boolean core, so every input still receives an output. The loss is not that a value is sent nowhere. The loss is expressive: the operation whose fixed point is the contradiction cannot be written inside the Boolean image.
331	
332	There is an intermediate version of the same construction. If only $B$ is removed and $\{N,T,F\}$ is kept, the resulting three-valued restriction is again a truth-order coreflection by the same two-case argument. Removing contradiction alone and removing both contradiction and incompleteness are therefore the same kind of construction at two different depths.
333	
334	Passing to a consistent world is consequently not a change of coordinates that richer bookkeeping could reverse. It removes a value for which the remaining structure has no name. The worked instance measures how much a specific paraconsistent object exceeds its classical retraction. That excess is positive at every finite size and shrinks toward the retraction as the size grows.
335	
336	\section{Tensor filtration}
337	
338	The central operation is the split together with its adjoint, the fuse. On a many-coordinate register these operations become a ladder of operators. We now build that ladder explicitly. It supplies the mechanics used both by measurement and by the worked instance.
339	
340	\subsection{The register and its weight sectors}
341	
342	Take $m$ copies of the constructive layer. This gives an $m$-coordinate register in which every coordinate carries one of the two one-sided values, $T$ or $F$. A basis state is therefore a string $x\in\{T,F\}^m$.
343	
344	The weight of such a string is the number of coordinates equal to $T$. All strings with the same weight belong to the same sector. The weight-$k$ sector contains $\binom{m}{k}$ strings.
345	
346	\begin{definition}[Uniform sector states]
347	For $0\leq k\leq m$ let $\ket{k} = \binom{m}{k}^{-1/2}\sum_{\mathrm{wt}(x)=k}\ket{x}$, the uniform normalized superposition of the weight-$k$ strings. The $\ket{k}$ are orthonormal and span the symmetric subspace, the part invariant under permuting coordinates.
348	\end{definition}
349	
350	\subsection{Canonical linearization of the register}
351	
352	The ket notation is not an added quantum postulate. It is the linear envelope of the finite register, constructed from the carrier rather than assumed.
353	
354	\begin{definition}[The register space]\label{def:lin}
355	Let $X_m = \{T,F\}^m$ be the constructive configurations of the $m$-coordinate register. Let $\mathcal{H}_m = \ell^2(X_m)$ be the free complex vector space on $X_m$, with basis $\ket{x}$ for $x\in X_m$ and inner product $\langle x\mid y\rangle = \delta_{xy}$. Every operation of the register extends linearly to $\mathcal{H}_m$.
356	\end{definition}
357	
358	The carrier already supplies two conditions that fix this inner product up to an overall scale. First, distinct classical configurations are perfectly distinguishable, so their basis vectors are orthogonal. Second, permuting coordinates cannot change the norm of a configuration. Once one basis state is normalized, all of them are normalized in the same way.
359	
360	The complex coefficient does not introduce a new object. The grammar already carries a winding, and a winding leaves the constructive support of a configuration unchanged. In the linear envelope, that unchanged support is represented by multiplication by a scalar of unit modulus. For a discrete winding group the scalar is a root of unity in the corresponding cyclotomic field. That field carries exact arithmetic content rather than merely another notation for phase: in the Fibonacci representation it constrains the spectrum of every braid word even though the braid image is dense, so a phase may be arbitrarily approximable while remaining absent from the exact image~\cite{gateobstruction}. Its continuous completion is the phase $e^{i\varphi}$.
361	
362	With this linearization in place, split and fuse become $S_{+}$ and $S_{-}=S_{+}^{\dagger}$. The uniform sector states of Definition~\ref{def:lin} above are exactly the vectors of $\mathcal{H}_m$ that remain unchanged when the coordinates are permuted.
363	
364	\subsection{Split as raising, fuse as lowering}
365	
366	\begin{theorem}[The ladder operators]\label{thm:ladder}
367	Let $S_{+}$ send a weight-$k$ string to the sum of its weight-$(k+1)$ extensions, each raising one coordinate from $F$ to $T$, and let $S_{-}$ be its adjoint. Then
368	\[
369	S_{+}\ket{k} = \sqrt{(k+1)(m-k)}\,\ket{k+1},
370	\qquad
371	S_{-}\ket{k} = \sqrt{k(m-k+1)}\,\ket{k-1}.
372	\]
373	\end{theorem}
374	
375	\begin{proof}
376	Acting with $S_{+}$, each weight-$k$ string $x$ maps to the sum of the $m-k$ strings obtained by raising one of its $F$ coordinates, so $S_{+}\ket{k} = \binom{m}{k}^{-1/2}\sum_{\mathrm{wt}(x)=k}\sum_{i:x_i=F}\ket{x^{(i)}}$. Each weight-$(k+1)$ string $y$ arises once for each of its $k+1$ coordinates at $T$, since lowering any one gives a weight-$k$ string that raises back to $y$, so the coefficient of $\ket{y}$ is $(k+1)\binom{m}{k}^{-1/2}$, and $S_{+}\ket{k} = (k+1)\binom{m}{k}^{-1/2}\binom{m}{k+1}^{1/2}\ket{k+1}$. With $\binom{m}{k+1}/\binom{m}{k} = (m-k)/(k+1)$ the coefficient is $\sqrt{(k+1)(m-k)}$. The adjoint gives $\langle k-1\mid S_{-}\mid k\rangle = \langle k\mid S_{+}\mid k-1\rangle = \sqrt{k(m-k+1)}$, so $S_{-}\ket{k}=\sqrt{k(m-k+1)}\ket{k-1}$.
377	\end{proof}
378	
379	The split is $S_{+}$, opening one coordinate into truth support; the fuse is $S_{-}$, its adjoint, closing one back.
380	
381	\begin{corollary}[The composite is a positive invertible diagonal, not the identity]\label{cor:comp}
382	On the sector basis,
383	\[
384	S_{-}S_{+}\ket{k} = (k+1)(m-k)\,\ket{k}, \qquad
385	S_{+}S_{-}\ket{k} = k(m-k+1)\,\ket{k},
386	\]
387	and the commutator is $[S_{+},S_{-}]\ket{k} = (2k-m)\ket{k}$, so $S_{+}$, $S_{-}$, and the weight operator generate the angular-momentum algebra of spin $m/2$. Fuse after split is not the identity; it is the positive diagonal $(k+1)(m-k)$, which is nonzero for every interior sector $0\leq k<m$, so $S_{+}$ is injective there and $S_{-}$ recovers $\ket{k}$ from $S_{+}\ket{k}$ up to that scalar.
388	\end{corollary}
389	
390	\begin{proof}
391	Apply Theorem~\ref{thm:ladder} twice:
392	\[
393	S_{-}S_{+}\ket{k}
394	= \sqrt{(k+1)(m-k)}\;S_{-}\ket{k+1}
395	= \Bigl(\sqrt{(k+1)(m-k)}\Bigr)^{2}\ket{k}
396	= (k+1)(m-k)\,\ket{k},
397	\]
398	and symmetrically $S_{+}S_{-}\ket{k} = k(m-k+1)\ket{k}$. Subtracting, $k(m-k+1)-(k+1)(m-k) = 2k-m$. The coefficient $(k+1)(m-k)$ vanishes only at $k=-1$ and $k=m$, outside the interior, so it is positive on every interior sector.
399	\end{proof}
400	
401	On the ladder, reversibility means invertibility: the original value can be recovered from its image. That is not the same claim as saying that the composite is literally the identity. Literal identity belongs to a different register-level description, treated in Section 5.4.
402	
403	\subsection{Evaluate as the diagonal, and the tridiagonal ladder}
404	
405	\begin{proposition}[The diagonal operator]\label{prop:diag}
406	For a fixed real $d$ let $D$ read $d$ once per coordinate at $T$. Then $D\ket{k}=kd\,\ket{k}$, diagonal in the sector basis.
407	\end{proposition}
408	
409	\begin{proof}
410	On a weight-$k$ string $D$ reads $d$ exactly $k$ times, so $D\ket{x}=kd\ket{x}$, and every string in $\ket{k}$ has weight $k$, so $D\ket{k}=kd\ket{k}$.
411	\end{proof}
412	
413	\begin{theorem}[The filtration matrix]\label{thm:matrix}
414	On the span of $\ket{0},\dots,\ket{\ell}$ the operator $A = D + S_{+} + S_{-}$ is the $(\ell+1)\times(\ell+1)$ real symmetric tridiagonal matrix with $A_{kk}=kd$ and $A_{k,k+1}=A_{k+1,k}=\sqrt{(k+1)(m-k)}$.
415	\end{theorem}
416	
417	\begin{proof}
418	The diagonal is $D$ by Proposition~\ref{prop:diag}. The first off-diagonals are the raising and lowering matrix elements of Theorem~\ref{thm:ladder}, equal, so symmetric. All other entries vanish because $S_{\pm}$ change the weight by one and $D$ preserves it.
419	\end{proof}
420	
421	The case $\ell=2$ shows the structure without the general notation. The matrix is
422	\[
423	A = \begin{pmatrix}
424	0 & \sqrt{m} & 0 \\
425	\sqrt{m} & d & \sqrt{2(m-1)} \\
426	0 & \sqrt{2(m-1)} & 2d
427	\end{pmatrix},
428	\]
429	Here the diagonal entries $0,d,2d$ count truth support in the three sectors. The first off-diagonal is $\sqrt{m}$ and the second is $\sqrt{2(m-1)}$, exactly the corresponding raising and lowering coefficients. Nothing else acts. Split and fuse move only between adjacent weights, while evaluate reads the weight of the sector already occupied.
430	
431	\begin{figure}[t]
432	\centering
433	\begin{tikzpicture}[vox]
434	  \foreach \k/\y in {0/0,1/1.9,2/3.8,3/5.7,4/7.6} {
435	    \node[voxstate] (k\k) at (0,\y) {$|\k\rangle$};
436	    \draw[voxarrow] (k\k.east) -- ++(34mm,0)
437	      node[right, font=\scriptsize] {$D|\k\rangle=\k d\,|\k\rangle$};
438	  }
439	
440	  \foreach \a/\b/\lab in {
441	    0/1/{\sqrt{1(m)}},
442	    1/2/{\sqrt{2(m-1)}},
443	    2/3/{\sqrt{3(m-2)}},
444	    3/4/{\sqrt{4(m-3)}}
445	  } {
446	    \draw[voxarrow, bend left=45] (k\a) to
447	      node[voxlabel,right,xshift=1mm] {$S_+$} (k\b);
448	    \draw[voxarrow, bend left=45] (k\b) to
449	      node[voxlabel,left,xshift=-1mm] {$S_-$} (k\a);
450	  }
451	
452	  \node[left=24mm of k2, align=center, font=\scriptsize]
453	    {split / raise};
454	  \node[right=58mm of k2, align=center, font=\scriptsize]
455	    {evaluate};
456	  \node[below=8mm of k0, align=center, font=\scriptsize]
457	    {$S_+|k\rangle=\sqrt{(k+1)(m-k)}\,|k+1\rangle,\quad S_-=S_+^\dagger$\\[1mm]
458	     $[S_+,S_-]|k\rangle=(2k-m)|k\rangle$};
459	\end{tikzpicture}
460	\caption{Tensor filtration as a weight-sector ladder. Split raises, fuse lowers, and evaluate reads the occupied sector in place. The same operators generate the spin-$m/2$ angular-momentum algebra.}
461	\label{fig:filtration-ladder}
462	\end{figure}
463	
464	\subsection{Two levels of reversibility: carrier identity, ladder invertibility}
465	
466	Split and fuse are realized differently on different registers. Their reversibility therefore appears in two forms. These are not competing descriptions of split and fuse. They are the same operations acting on two different registers, and keeping those two realizations distinct is the point of this subsection.
467	
468	On a single-value register, split is the copy map $\delta(x)=(x,x)$ and fuse is the join $\mu(x,y)=x\vee_{\mathrm i}y$. Because the join is idempotent, fuse after split is literally the identity on that register,
469	\[
470	\mu(\delta(x)) = x\vee_{\mathrm i}x = x,
471	\]
472	which is the counit-unit law of a Frobenius comonoid. This is the literal identity used by the worked program of Section 2.5. It splits $N$ into $(N,N)$, deposits nothing on the reverse pass, and fuses the two copies back to $N$. At this same single-value level, the Frobenius compatibility that lets fuse slide across split is
473	\[
474	(\mu\otimes\mathrm{id})\circ(\mathrm{id}\otimes\delta)
475	= \delta\circ\mu
476	= (\mathrm{id}\otimes\mu)\circ(\delta\otimes\mathrm{id}),
477	\]
478	holds at this level as well.
479	
480	On the $m$-fold register, the same operations have a different realization. They act as the raising and lowering operators $S_{+}$ and $S_{-}$ of Theorem~\ref{thm:ladder}. In this register, fuse after split is not the identity. Corollary~\ref{cor:comp} shows that the composite is the positive diagonal $(k+1)(m-k)$, while $S_{+}$, $S_{-}$, and the weight operator generate the angular-momentum algebra of spin $m/2$.
481	
482	Reversibility here means invertibility. The diagonal is nonzero on every interior sector, so $S_{+}$ is injective there and the original value can be recovered from its image without information loss. The distinction between the two registers is exact. On the single-value register, copy followed by join is the identity. On the $m$-fold register, raising followed by lowering is a positive invertible diagonal. Both descriptions are reversible, but only the first composite is literally the identity.
483	
484	The operator $A = D + S_{+} + S_{-}$ is self-adjoint by Theorem~\ref{thm:matrix}, so the motion it generates, $e^{-iAt}$, is unitary and hence invertible, the register-level reversibility of the whole filtration. The self-reference operation is the one true identity operator on a value, distinct from either composite above; it is the fixed point the reversibility turns around.
485	
486	\subsection{The single-coordinate orbit is the qubit sphere}
487	
488	The ladder already carries the geometry the Born rule needs. We make the angular-momentum structure explicit and read off its orbit.
489	
490	\begin{proposition}[The su(2) action of the ladder]\label{prop:su2}
491	Let $K\ket{k}=k\ket{k}$ be the weight operator and set
492	\[
493	J_z = K - \tfrac{m}{2}I, \qquad J_{+}=S_{+}, \qquad J_{-}=S_{-},
494	\qquad J_x = \tfrac{J_{+}+J_{-}}{2}, \qquad J_y = \tfrac{J_{+}-J_{-}}{2i}.
495	\]
496	Then $[J_z,J_{\pm}]=\pm J_{\pm}$ and $[J_{+},J_{-}]=2J_z$, so $[J_x,J_y]=iJ_z$, $[J_y,J_z]=iJ_x$, $[J_z,J_x]=iJ_y$: the ladder generates the su(2) action of spin $m/2$. For $m=1$, in the ordered basis $(\ket{T},\ket{F})$, $J_x=\tfrac12\sigma_x$, $J_y=\tfrac12\sigma_y$, $J_z=\tfrac12\sigma_z$.
497	\end{proposition}
498	
499	\begin{proof}
500	Because $S_{+}$ raises the weight by one and $S_{-}$ lowers it by one, $[J_z,J_{\pm}]=\pm J_{\pm}$ directly. The third relation is Corollary~\ref{cor:comp}, $[S_{+},S_{-}]\ket{k}=(2k-m)\ket{k}=2J_z\ket{k}$. The Cartesian commutators follow by substitution. At $m=1$ the weight-$1$ state is $\ket{T}$ and the weight-$0$ state $\ket{F}$; then $J_z=\mathrm{diag}(\tfrac12,-\tfrac12)=\tfrac12\sigma_z$, $J_{+}=\ket{T}\bra{F}$, $J_{-}=\ket{F}\bra{T}$, giving $J_x=\tfrac12\sigma_x$ and $J_y=\tfrac12\sigma_y$.
501	\end{proof}
502	
503	\begin{proposition}[The qubit sphere is the single-coordinate orbit]\label{prop:sphere}
504	The unitary changes of frame $U(\mathbf{n},\theta)=e^{-i\theta\,\mathbf{n}\cdot\mathbf{J}}$ act on the $m=1$ register. The stabilizer of the ray $[\ket{T}]$ is the phase subgroup $U(1)$, so the orbit of a classical pole is $SU(2)/U(1)\cong\mathbb{CP}^1\cong S^2$. Acting on the truth pole gives, up to a common phase,
505	\[
506	\ket{\psi(\theta,\varphi)} = \cos\tfrac{\theta}{2}\ket{T} + e^{i\varphi}\sin\tfrac{\theta}{2}\ket{F},
507	\]
508	and the half-angle is forced by the spin one-half representation the ladder generates.
509	\end{proposition}
510	
511	The qubit state space is therefore the ray space of the single-coordinate split-fuse representation. In this account it is obtained from the ladder rather than introduced as an independent assumption. Section 6.2 uses this derived sphere for the Born rule. The additional ingredients supplied there are positivity and additivity.
512	
513	\begin{figure}[t]
514	\centering
515	\resizebox{\linewidth}{!}{%
516	\begin{tikzpicture}[vox, node distance=30mm]
517	  \node[voxbox, minimum width=22mm] (carrier) {$\{T,F\}$\\register};
518	  \node[voxbox, minimum width=28mm, right=of carrier] (lin)
519	    {$\ell^2(\{T,F\})$\\linear envelope};
520	  \node[voxbox, minimum width=26mm, right=of lin] (alg)
521	    {$S_\pm,K$\\$su(2)$};
522	  \node[voxbox, minimum width=30mm, right=of alg] (orbit)
523	    {$SU(2)/U(1)$\\$\cong\mathbb{CP}^{1}\cong S^2$};
524	  \node[voxbox, minimum width=30mm, right=of orbit] (born)
525	    {$P_T=|T\rangle\!\langle T|$\\$p_T=\cos^2(\theta/2)$};
526	
527	  \draw[voxarrow] (carrier) -- node[voxlabel,above] {distinguishability} (lin);
528	  \draw[voxarrow] (lin) -- node[voxlabel,above] {split/fuse} (alg);
529	  \draw[voxarrow] (alg) -- node[voxlabel,above] {$m=1$ orbit} (orbit);
530	  \draw[voxarrow] (orbit) -- node[voxlabel,above] {positive reading} (born);
531	
532	  \node[below=7mm of alg, font=\scriptsize, align=center]
533	    {$J_z=K-\tfrac12I$,\quad
534	      $J_x=\tfrac12(S_++S_-)$,\quad
535	      $J_y=\tfrac1{2i}(S_+-S_-)$};
536	\end{tikzpicture}}
537	\caption{A dependency diagram for the qubit geometry. The projective sphere and the quadratic Born weight arise from the single-coordinate split--fuse representation after canonical linearization, rather than being introduced as an independent ontology.}
538	\label{fig:born-derivation}
539	\end{figure}
540	
541	\subsection{Measurement is filtration through the ladder}
542	
543	Measurement selects a sector of the ladder. Split opens the register across the ladder, a chosen sector is read, and fuse closes the register again. Because fuse is the adjoint of split, the ladder itself persists through the reading.
544	
545	A classical description records this process as a collapse to one definite weight. In the ladder description, however, what has happened is that one rung has been read while the ladder remains present. The unread rungs are the tensor factors opened by split and available to fuse for recovery. Measurement is therefore a filtration that selects one rung, followed by a fuse that can recover the whole.
546	
547	The apparent destruction of the other rungs occurs only in the classical slice, which cannot carry more than the rung it records. This is the register-level form of the inexpressibility proved in Theorem~\ref{thm:lossy}.
548	
549	The word tensor is meant literally. Split opens a value into tensor factors. A reading selects one factor while the other remains part of the whole, and fuse recombines them. Reading $x\otimes y$ therefore reads $x$ without deleting $y$.
550	
551	A genuine collapse would instead destroy the unread factor. That destruction would be an erasure with a Landauer cost~\cite{landauer}. In the reversible-computing models of Bennett~\cite{bennett} and of Fredkin and Toffoli~\cite{fredkin}, a computation built entirely from reversible operations has no such erasure cost. Filtration erases no factor, and fuse restores what split opened. The ambient therefore pays no thermodynamic price for measurement. The cost appears only in the classical image, which keeps one factor and discards the other.
552	
553	\begin{figure}[t]
554	\centering
555	\resizebox{\linewidth}{!}{%
556	\begin{tikzpicture}[vox, node distance=22mm]
557	  \node[voxbox] (x) {$x$};
558	  \node[voxbox, right=of x] (split) {$\delta(x)$\\$x\otimes x$};
559	  \node[voxbox, right=of split, minimum width=31mm] (read)
560	    {read one factor\\$P_k(x\otimes x)$};
561	  \node[voxbox, right=of read] (fuse) {$\mu$\\fuse};
562	  \node[voxbox, right=of fuse] (recover) {$x$\\recovered};
