\title{\bfseries Upending Quantum Mechanics\\[0.3em]
\large Tensor Filtration and the Paraconsistent Ambient}
\author{C. Lando Mills\\ \small Trabuco Canyon, California}
\date{3 September 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
\noindent
Quantum mechanics is the classical-logical image of a paraconsistent four-valued logic. On this account, the ambient structure is the paraconsistent trilattice of sixteen values. The consistent classical world is a restriction of that structure, reached by two retractions that discard the states carrying contradiction and incompleteness.

This paper develops the mechanics of that reading and grounds it in a complete worked instance. We first build the carrier explicitly, including its three orders, its negation, and its consequence relation. We then prove that the classical Boolean values form a proper part of the carrier, reached by two distinct adjunctions, ex falso and ex quodlibet, and that the paraconsistent value cannot be expressed inside either classical image.

The operation at the center of the account is tensor filtration. We realize it as an explicit ladder of raising, lowering, and diagonal operators on the weight sectors of a many-coordinate register, and show that measurement is filtration through this ladder rather than collapse.

We then give a full worked instance in which the classical image of a paraconsistent object is computed and measured. Optimal Polynomial Intersection has a quantum algorithm, Decoded Quantum Interferometry, whose expected performance is governed by the largest eigenvalue of a finite tridiagonal matrix. We show that this matrix is the ladder of the split, fuse, and evaluate operations on a paraconsistent register, and that the algorithm's expectation value is that ladder read through the coreflector. Across four scales, we compute both the finite operator-valued value and its asymptotic scalar limit, and measure the richer finite description collapsing onto the poorer limit in exactly the direction the coreflector moves.
\end{abstract}

