\subsection{Three orders, three lattices}

\begin{definition}[The three orders]
For $x,y \in \mathbf{16}$: the information order $x \leqi y$ is $x \subseteq y$; the truth order $x \leqt y$ holds when $x \cap \{T,t\} \subseteq y \cap \{T,t\}$ and $y \cap \{F,f\} \subseteq x \cap \{F,f\}$; the constructivity order $x \leqc y$ holds when $x \cap \{T,F\} \subseteq y \cap \{T,F\}$ and $y \cap \{t,f\} \subseteq x \cap \{t,f\}$.
\end{definition}

The information order asks how much is known, and moving up it only adds marks. The truth order asks how true a value is, truth support growing and falsity support shrinking. The constructivity order asks how settled a value is, the constructive layer growing and the informational layer shrinking.

\begin{proposition}[Partial orders and lattices]\label{prop:orders}
Each of $\leqi$, $\leqt$, $\leqc$ is a partial order, and each is a lattice: the $\leqi$ join and meet are union and intersection; the $\leqt$ join takes the truth marks by union and the falsity marks by intersection, and its meet the reverse; the $\leqc$ join and meet are the same with the two halves $\{T,F\}$ and $\{t,f\}$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
For $\leqi$ this is the powerset order. For $\leqt$, write $u(x) = x\cap\{T,t\}$ and $v(x) = x\cap\{F,f\}$, so $x\leqt y$ is $u(x)\subseteq u(y)$ and $v(y)\subseteq v(x)$; reflexivity is immediate, transitivity follows from transitivity of $\subseteq$ in each half, and antisymmetry holds because $\{T,t\}$ and $\{F,f\}$ partition the marks so a value is fixed by its two halves. An upper bound $z$ of $x,y$ needs $u(z)\supseteq u(x)\cup u(y)$ and $v(z)\subseteq v(x)\cap v(y)$, and the value with equality in both is below every other upper bound, the join; the meet is dual. For $\leqc$ the argument is identical with the halves $\{T,F\}$ and $\{t,f\}$.
\end{proof}

On the Belnap four the truth order is $F \leqt N \leqt T$ and $F \leqt B \leqt T$ with $N,B$ incomparable, so $F$ is the bottom and $T$ the top. The truth-order meet and join on the Belnap four are

