so $B \wedge_{\mathrm t} \neg B = B \wedge_{\mathrm t} B = B \neq F$ and $B \vee_{\mathrm t} \neg B = B \neq T$: a contradiction met or joined with its own negation is the contradiction, held rather than forced to a pole.

\subsection{Negation and the operation set}

\begin{definition}[Negation]
Negation $\neg$ exchanges $T$ with $F$ and $t$ with $f$; in bits $\neg(a,b,c,d)=(b,a,d,c)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}[Two fixed points]\label{prop:neg}
$\neg$ is an involution reversing $\leqt$ and preserving $\leqi$, with fixed points exactly $N$ and $B$ on the Belnap four, while $\neg T = F$ and $\neg F = T$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Applying the swap twice restores each bit. Negation exchanges the two halves of the $\leqt$ condition, reversing that order, and permutes marks by presence, preserving $\subseteq$. Fixed points: $\neg\{\}=\{\}$ and $\neg\{T,F\}=\{F,T\}=\{T,F\}$, while $\neg\{T\}=\{F\}\neq\{T\}$.
\end{proof}

The two negation-stable values are the two nonclassical ones. Negation moves the consistent values and cannot move the undetermined or the overdetermined, the first sign of the asymmetry the paper turns on.

The operations on the carrier are drawn from a fixed set of twelve primitive operations. We name them here by their functional roles. The operations are: begin, close, forward, reverse, compose, self-reference, split, fuse, evaluate true, evaluate false, engage, and fix. A program over the carrier is a string of these twelve operations.

Three operations move the structural verdict of a program: the split opens a fork, the fuse closes one, and the close anchors the program. On a single value the split and fuse compose to the identity, since the split copies and the fuse joins and the join is idempotent; realized as ladder operators on a many-coordinate register they compose instead to a positive invertible diagonal, reversible by invertibility, and Section 5 carries both levels.

\subsection{Entailment without explosion}

The logic is called First-Degree Entailment because its consequence relation keeps contradictions local. For programs built from the connectives $\wedge_{\mathrm t}$, $\vee_{\mathrm t}$, $\neg$, write $A \models B$ when for every assignment of carrier values to the atoms, $\llbracket A \rrbracket \leqt \llbracket B \rrbracket$: the value of $A$ is at least as true as $B$ under every valuation.

\begin{theorem}[No explosion, no excluded middle]\label{thm:entail}
A contradiction does not entail an arbitrary conclusion, and a formula joined with its negation is not a tautology. There are assignments with $A \wedge_{\mathrm t} \neg A \not\models C$ and with $\llbracket A \vee_{\mathrm t} \neg A \rrbracket \neq T$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
For the first, take atoms $A,C$ and the assignment $A=B$, $C=F$; then $\llbracket A \wedge_{\mathrm t}\neg A\rrbracket = B\wedge_{\mathrm t}B = B$ while $\llbracket C\rrbracket = F$, and $B\not\leqt F$ since $F$ is the truth-order bottom. For the second, take $A=N$; then $N\vee_{\mathrm t}\neg N = N \neq T$, so $A\vee_{\mathrm t}\neg A$ is not a tautology.
\end{proof}

The value $B$ blocks explosion, taking a value above the truth-order bottom so a contradiction does not lie below every conclusion; the value $N$ blocks excluded middle. Removing both recovers classical consequence, and that removal is the restriction of the next section.

\subsection{Two programs evaluated}

A program over the carrier is evaluated by running its operations in order on an input value and inspecting the value that comes out. Two short examples make that mechanism explicit.

The first program is begin, split, evaluate true, evaluate false, fuse, close. Read from left to right, it says: begin, split the value into two arms, deposit truth support on one arm, deposit falsity support on the other, fuse the arms, and close.

Start with the undetermined value $N=\{\}$. The split opens $N$ into the pair $(N,N)$. Evaluate-true deposits $T$ on the first arm, producing $\{T\}$. Evaluate-false deposits $F$ on the second, producing $\{F\}$. The fuse then recombines the two arms by mark-wise union: $\{T\}\cup\{F\}=\{T,F\}=B$. The program therefore takes an undetermined input to the overdetermined value that holds both. This is the value called superposition in Section 6.1.

The forward program maps $N$ to $B$, so the forward program itself is not the identity. Its inverse is the reverse program with each operation inverted. That reverse program splits $B$ back into the arms previously joined by the fuse, clears the two deposits, and joins the emptied arms back to $N$. The starting value is therefore recovered, so the forward program followed by its inverse is the identity on $N$.

That recovery depends on the arms banked by the split. The join by itself is not injective. This is the banking form of invertibility developed in Section 5, and it must be kept distinct from the bare copy-then-join skeleton, which returns the value because the join is idempotent.

The second program is begin, evaluate true, fix, close: begin, deposit truth support, fix, and close. Start again at $N$. Evaluate-true produces $\{T\}=T$, which is a one-sided classical value. The operation fix then performs the irreversible commit. It records that value into the winding and closes.

The program cannot be run backward past the fix because the commit is the one operation with no adjoint. Its verdict is therefore a definite classical $T$ with no held part. This makes the contrast with the first example exact. The first program uses split and fuse, is reversible, and lands on the held value. The second uses a fix, is irreversible, and lands on a pole. Classical values are the outputs of programs that fix. The held value is the output of programs that only split and fuse.

\section{The classical restriction}

Passing to a consistent world means retracting the paraconsistent carrier onto its Boolean core. That retraction is lossy in a precise sense. This section proves the loss formally, and the worked instance later measures it.

