\begin{definition}[Boolean core and the two collapses]
Let $\mathbf{B}=\{F,T\}$ with inherited truth order and negation, the two-element Boolean algebra, exactly the Belnap values with $x\vee_{\mathrm t}\neg x = T$. Let the ex falso map $c$ and ex quodlibet map $r$ send $\{N,T,F,B\}$ to $\mathbf{B}$ by $c(T)=T,c(F)=F,c(N)=F,c(B)=F$ and $r(T)=T,r(F)=F,r(N)=T,r(B)=T$, and let $\iota$ be the inclusion.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Two distinct adjunctions]\label{thm:two}
$c$ is monotone, fixes $\mathbf{B}$, and is right adjoint to $\iota$ in the truth order, $\iota(x)\leqt v \iff x\leqt c(v)$. $r$ is monotone, fixes $\mathbf{B}$, and is left adjoint, $r(v)\leqt x \iff v\leqt\iota(x)$. They differ on the nonclassical values, $r(N)=r(B)=T\neq F=c(N)=c(B)$, and agree on $\mathbf{B}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The truth-order covers are $F\leqt N$, $F\leqt B$, $N\leqt T$, $B\leqt T$. Under $c$ they map to $F\leqt F,F\leqt F,F\leqt T,F\leqt T$, all true, so $c$ is monotone. The adjunction, two cases: for $x=F$ both $x\leqt c(v)$ and $\iota(x)\leqt v$ hold for all $v$ since $F$ is the bottom; for $x=T$, $x\leqt c(v)$ forces $c(v)=T$, holding exactly at $v=T$, and $\iota(T)\leqt v$ forces $v=T$. Under $r$ the covers map to $F\leqt T,F\leqt T,T\leqt T,T\leqt T$, all true; for $x=T$ both sides hold for all $v$, and for $x=F$, $r(v)\leqt F$ forces $r(v)=F$ at exactly $v=F$, while $v\leqt\iota(F)=F$ forces $v=F$. Distinctness is direct.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Neither is an information-order adjunction]\label{prop:noinfo}
In the information order, where $N$ is the bottom and $B$ the top, neither $c$ nor $r$ is adjoint to $\iota$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Were $r$ left adjoint in $\leqi$, then $v=B$, $x=T$ would need $r(B)\leqi T \iff B\leqi\iota(T)=T$; the right side is false ($B$ is the top), the left side $T\leqi T$ true. Were $c$ right adjoint, $v=N$, $x=F$ would need $\iota(F)=F\leqi N \iff F\leqi c(N)=F$; the left side is false ($N$ is the bottom), the right true.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The collapse is exactly lossy]\label{thm:lossy}
Let $\mathrm{inc}$ send every Belnap value to $B$. It is idempotent with unique fixed point $B$, and there is no map $\varphi:\mathbf{B}\to\mathbf{B}$ with $\iota\circ\varphi = \mathrm{inc}\circ\iota$: the value $B$ is inexpressible inside either classical image.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
$\mathrm{inc}(\mathrm{inc}(x))=\mathrm{inc}(B)=B=\mathrm{inc}(x)$, and a fixed point solves $x=B$. If $\varphi$ existed, $\iota(\varphi(x))=\mathrm{inc}(\iota(x))=B$ for classical $x$; but $\iota$ has image $\{F,T\}$, which excludes $B$, so no value of $\varphi(x)$ maps to $B$.
\end{proof}

Each retraction is a total map onto the Boolean core, so every input still receives an output. The loss is not that a value is sent nowhere. The loss is expressive: the operation whose fixed point is the contradiction cannot be written inside the Boolean image.

There is an intermediate version of the same construction. If only $B$ is removed and $\{N,T,F\}$ is kept, the resulting three-valued restriction is again a truth-order coreflection by the same two-case argument. Removing contradiction alone and removing both contradiction and incompleteness are therefore the same kind of construction at two different depths.

Passing to a consistent world is consequently not a change of coordinates that richer bookkeeping could reverse. It removes a value for which the remaining structure has no name. The worked instance measures how much a specific paraconsistent object exceeds its classical retraction. That excess is positive at every finite size and shrinks toward the retraction as the size grows.

\section{Tensor filtration}

The central operation is the split together with its adjoint, the fuse. On a many-coordinate register these operations become a ladder of operators. We now build that ladder explicitly. It supplies the mechanics used both by measurement and by the worked instance.

\subsection{The register and its weight sectors}

Take $m$ copies of the constructive layer. This gives an $m$-coordinate register in which every coordinate carries one of the two one-sided values, $T$ or $F$. A basis state is therefore a string $x\in\{T,F\}^m$.

The weight of such a string is the number of coordinates equal to $T$. All strings with the same weight belong to the same sector. The weight-$k$ sector contains $\binom{m}{k}$ strings.

\begin{definition}[Uniform sector states]
For $0\leq k\leq m$ let $\ket{k} = \binom{m}{k}^{-1/2}\sum_{\mathrm{wt}(x)=k}\ket{x}$, the uniform normalized superposition of the weight-$k$ strings. The $\ket{k}$ are orthonormal and span the symmetric subspace, the part invariant under permuting coordinates.
\end{definition}

\subsection{Canonical linearization of the register}

The ket notation is not an added quantum postulate. It is the linear envelope of the finite register, constructed from the carrier rather than assumed.

\begin{definition}[The register space]\label{def:lin}
Let $X_m = \{T,F\}^m$ be the constructive configurations of the $m$-coordinate register. Let $\mathcal{H}_m = \ell^2(X_m)$ be the free complex vector space on $X_m$, with basis $\ket{x}$ for $x\in X_m$ and inner product $\langle x\mid y\rangle = \delta_{xy}$. Every operation of the register extends linearly to $\mathcal{H}_m$.
\end{definition}

The carrier already supplies two conditions that fix this inner product up to an overall scale. First, distinct classical configurations are perfectly distinguishable, so their basis vectors are orthogonal. Second, permuting coordinates cannot change the norm of a configuration. Once one basis state is normalized, all of them are normalized in the same way.

The complex coefficient does not introduce a new object. The grammar already carries a winding, and a winding leaves the constructive support of a configuration unchanged. In the linear envelope, that unchanged support is represented by multiplication by a scalar of unit modulus. For a discrete winding group the scalar is a root of unity in the corresponding cyclotomic field. That field carries exact arithmetic content rather than merely another notation for phase: in the Fibonacci representation it constrains the spectrum of every braid word even though the braid image is dense, so a phase may be arbitrarily approximable while remaining absent from the exact image~\cite{gateobstruction}. Its continuous completion is the phase $e^{i\varphi}$.

With this linearization in place, split and fuse become $S_{+}$ and $S_{-}=S_{+}^{\dagger}$. The uniform sector states of Definition~\ref{def:lin} above are exactly the vectors of $\mathcal{H}_m$ that remain unchanged when the coordinates are permuted.

\subsection{Split as raising, fuse as lowering}

\begin{theorem}[The ladder operators]\label{thm:ladder}
Let $S_{+}$ send a weight-$k$ string to the sum of its weight-$(k+1)$ extensions, each raising one coordinate from $F$ to $T$, and let $S_{-}$ be its adjoint. Then
\[
S_{+}\ket{k} = \sqrt{(k+1)(m-k)}\,\ket{k+1},
\qquad
S_{-}\ket{k} = \sqrt{k(m-k+1)}\,\ket{k-1}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Acting with $S_{+}$, each weight-$k$ string $x$ maps to the sum of the $m-k$ strings obtained by raising one of its $F$ coordinates, so $S_{+}\ket{k} = \binom{m}{k}^{-1/2}\sum_{\mathrm{wt}(x)=k}\sum_{i:x_i=F}\ket{x^{(i)}}$. Each weight-$(k+1)$ string $y$ arises once for each of its $k+1$ coordinates at $T$, since lowering any one gives a weight-$k$ string that raises back to $y$, so the coefficient of $\ket{y}$ is $(k+1)\binom{m}{k}^{-1/2}$, and $S_{+}\ket{k} = (k+1)\binom{m}{k}^{-1/2}\binom{m}{k+1}^{1/2}\ket{k+1}$. With $\binom{m}{k+1}/\binom{m}{k} = (m-k)/(k+1)$ the coefficient is $\sqrt{(k+1)(m-k)}$. The adjoint gives $\langle k-1\mid S_{-}\mid k\rangle = \langle k\mid S_{+}\mid k-1\rangle = \sqrt{k(m-k+1)}$, so $S_{-}\ket{k}=\sqrt{k(m-k+1)}\ket{k-1}$.
\end{proof}

The split is $S_{+}$, opening one coordinate into truth support; the fuse is $S_{-}$, its adjoint, closing one back.

\begin{corollary}[The composite is a positive invertible diagonal, not the identity]\label{cor:comp}
On the sector basis,
\[
S_{-}S_{+}\ket{k} = (k+1)(m-k)\,\ket{k}, \qquad
S_{+}S_{-}\ket{k} = k(m-k+1)\,\ket{k},
\]
and the commutator is $[S_{+},S_{-}]\ket{k} = (2k-m)\ket{k}$, so $S_{+}$, $S_{-}$, and the weight operator generate the angular-momentum algebra of spin $m/2$. Fuse after split is not the identity; it is the positive diagonal $(k+1)(m-k)$, which is nonzero for every interior sector $0\leq k<m$, so $S_{+}$ is injective there and $S_{-}$ recovers $\ket{k}$ from $S_{+}\ket{k}$ up to that scalar.
\end{corollary}

\begin{proof}
Apply Theorem~\ref{thm:ladder} twice:
\[
S_{-}S_{+}\ket{k}
= \sqrt{(k+1)(m-k)}\;S_{-}\ket{k+1}
= \Bigl(\sqrt{(k+1)(m-k)}\Bigr)^{2}\ket{k}
= (k+1)(m-k)\,\ket{k},
\]
and symmetrically $S_{+}S_{-}\ket{k} = k(m-k+1)\ket{k}$. Subtracting, $k(m-k+1)-(k+1)(m-k) = 2k-m$. The coefficient $(k+1)(m-k)$ vanishes only at $k=-1$ and $k=m$, outside the interior, so it is positive on every interior sector.
\end{proof}

On the ladder, reversibility means invertibility: the original value can be recovered from its image. That is not the same claim as saying that the composite is literally the identity. Literal identity belongs to a different register-level description, treated in Section 5.4.

\subsection{Evaluate as the diagonal, and the tridiagonal ladder}

\begin{proposition}[The diagonal operator]\label{prop:diag}
For a fixed real $d$ let $D$ read $d$ once per coordinate at $T$. Then $D\ket{k}=kd\,\ket{k}$, diagonal in the sector basis.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
On a weight-$k$ string $D$ reads $d$ exactly $k$ times, so $D\ket{x}=kd\ket{x}$, and every string in $\ket{k}$ has weight $k$, so $D\ket{k}=kd\ket{k}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The filtration matrix]\label{thm:matrix}
On the span of $\ket{0},\dots,\ket{\ell}$ the operator $A = D + S_{+} + S_{-}$ is the $(\ell+1)\times(\ell+1)$ real symmetric tridiagonal matrix with $A_{kk}=kd$ and $A_{k,k+1}=A_{k+1,k}=\sqrt{(k+1)(m-k)}$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The diagonal is $D$ by Proposition~\ref{prop:diag}. The first off-diagonals are the raising and lowering matrix elements of Theorem~\ref{thm:ladder}, equal, so symmetric. All other entries vanish because $S_{\pm}$ change the weight by one and $D$ preserves it.
\end{proof}

The case $\ell=2$ shows the structure without the general notation. The matrix is
\[
A = \begin{pmatrix}
0 & \sqrt{m} & 0 \\
\sqrt{m} & d & \sqrt{2(m-1)} \\
0 & \sqrt{2(m-1)} & 2d
\end{pmatrix},
\]
Here the diagonal entries $0,d,2d$ count truth support in the three sectors. The first off-diagonal is $\sqrt{m}$ and the second is $\sqrt{2(m-1)}$, exactly the corresponding raising and lowering coefficients. Nothing else acts. Split and fuse move only between adjacent weights, while evaluate reads the weight of the sector already occupied.

