\subsection{Two levels of reversibility: carrier identity, ladder invertibility}

Split and fuse are realized differently on different registers. Their reversibility therefore appears in two forms. These are not competing descriptions of split and fuse. They are the same operations acting on two different registers, and keeping those two realizations distinct is the point of this subsection.

On a single-value register, split is the copy map $\delta(x)=(x,x)$ and fuse is the join $\mu(x,y)=x\vee_{\mathrm i}y$. Because the join is idempotent, fuse after split is literally the identity on that register,
\[
\mu(\delta(x)) = x\vee_{\mathrm i}x = x,
\]
which is the counit-unit law of a Frobenius comonoid. This is the literal identity used by the worked program of Section 2.5. It splits $N$ into $(N,N)$, deposits nothing on the reverse pass, and fuses the two copies back to $N$. At this same single-value level, the Frobenius compatibility that lets fuse slide across split is
\[
(\mu\otimes\mathrm{id})\circ(\mathrm{id}\otimes\delta)
= \delta\circ\mu
= (\mathrm{id}\otimes\mu)\circ(\delta\otimes\mathrm{id}),
\]
holds at this level as well.

On the $m$-fold register, the same operations have a different realization. They act as the raising and lowering operators $S_{+}$ and $S_{-}$ of Theorem~\ref{thm:ladder}. In this register, fuse after split is not the identity. Corollary~\ref{cor:comp} shows that the composite is the positive diagonal $(k+1)(m-k)$, while $S_{+}$, $S_{-}$, and the weight operator generate the angular-momentum algebra of spin $m/2$.

Reversibility here means invertibility. The diagonal is nonzero on every interior sector, so $S_{+}$ is injective there and the original value can be recovered from its image without information loss. The distinction between the two registers is exact. On the single-value register, copy followed by join is the identity. On the $m$-fold register, raising followed by lowering is a positive invertible diagonal. Both descriptions are reversible, but only the first composite is literally the identity.

The operator $A = D + S_{+} + S_{-}$ is self-adjoint by Theorem~\ref{thm:matrix}, so the motion it generates, $e^{-iAt}$, is unitary and hence invertible, the register-level reversibility of the whole filtration. The self-reference operation is the one true identity operator on a value, distinct from either composite above; it is the fixed point the reversibility turns around.

\subsection{The single-coordinate orbit is the qubit sphere}

The ladder already carries the geometry the Born rule needs. We make the angular-momentum structure explicit and read off its orbit.

\begin{proposition}[The su(2) action of the ladder]\label{prop:su2}
Let $K\ket{k}=k\ket{k}$ be the weight operator and set
\[
J_z = K - \tfrac{m}{2}I, \qquad J_{+}=S_{+}, \qquad J_{-}=S_{-},
\qquad J_x = \tfrac{J_{+}+J_{-}}{2}, \qquad J_y = \tfrac{J_{+}-J_{-}}{2i}.
\]
Then $[J_z,J_{\pm}]=\pm J_{\pm}$ and $[J_{+},J_{-}]=2J_z$, so $[J_x,J_y]=iJ_z$, $[J_y,J_z]=iJ_x$, $[J_z,J_x]=iJ_y$: the ladder generates the su(2) action of spin $m/2$. For $m=1$, in the ordered basis $(\ket{T},\ket{F})$, $J_x=\tfrac12\sigma_x$, $J_y=\tfrac12\sigma_y$, $J_z=\tfrac12\sigma_z$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Because $S_{+}$ raises the weight by one and $S_{-}$ lowers it by one, $[J_z,J_{\pm}]=\pm J_{\pm}$ directly. The third relation is Corollary~\ref{cor:comp}, $[S_{+},S_{-}]\ket{k}=(2k-m)\ket{k}=2J_z\ket{k}$. The Cartesian commutators follow by substitution. At $m=1$ the weight-$1$ state is $\ket{T}$ and the weight-$0$ state $\ket{F}$; then $J_z=\mathrm{diag}(\tfrac12,-\tfrac12)=\tfrac12\sigma_z$, $J_{+}=\ket{T}\bra{F}$, $J_{-}=\ket{F}\bra{T}$, giving $J_x=\tfrac12\sigma_x$ and $J_y=\tfrac12\sigma_y$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[The qubit sphere is the single-coordinate orbit]\label{prop:sphere}
The unitary changes of frame $U(\mathbf{n},\theta)=e^{-i\theta\,\mathbf{n}\cdot\mathbf{J}}$ act on the $m=1$ register. The stabilizer of the ray $[\ket{T}]$ is the phase subgroup $U(1)$, so the orbit of a classical pole is $SU(2)/U(1)\cong\mathbb{CP}^1\cong S^2$. Acting on the truth pole gives, up to a common phase,
\[
\ket{\psi(\theta,\varphi)} = \cos\tfrac{\theta}{2}\ket{T} + e^{i\varphi}\sin\tfrac{\theta}{2}\ket{F},
\]
and the half-angle is forced by the spin one-half representation the ladder generates.
\end{proposition}

The qubit state space is therefore the ray space of the single-coordinate split-fuse representation. In this account it is obtained from the ladder rather than introduced as an independent assumption. Section 6.2 uses this derived sphere for the Born rule. The additional ingredients supplied there are positivity and additivity.

