\subsection{Measurement is filtration through the ladder}

Measurement selects a sector of the ladder. Split opens the register across the ladder, a chosen sector is read, and fuse closes the register again. Because fuse is the adjoint of split, the ladder itself persists through the reading.

A classical description records this process as a collapse to one definite weight. In the ladder description, however, what has happened is that one rung has been read while the ladder remains present. The unread rungs are the tensor factors opened by split and available to fuse for recovery. Measurement is therefore a filtration that selects one rung, followed by a fuse that can recover the whole.

The apparent destruction of the other rungs occurs only in the classical slice, which cannot carry more than the rung it records. This is the register-level form of the inexpressibility proved in Theorem~\ref{thm:lossy}.

The word tensor is meant literally. Split opens a value into tensor factors. A reading selects one factor while the other remains part of the whole, and fuse recombines them. Reading $x\otimes y$ therefore reads $x$ without deleting $y$.

A genuine collapse would instead destroy the unread factor. That destruction would be an erasure with a Landauer cost~\cite{landauer}. In the reversible-computing models of Bennett~\cite{bennett} and of Fredkin and Toffoli~\cite{fredkin}, a computation built entirely from reversible operations has no such erasure cost. Filtration erases no factor, and fuse restores what split opened. The ambient therefore pays no thermodynamic price for measurement. The cost appears only in the classical image, which keeps one factor and discards the other.

