\section{The phenomena}

We can now read the standard phenomena of quantum mechanics in the language of the carrier and the ladder. In each case, the familiar quantum description is the classical image of a corresponding feature of the ambient structure.

\subsection{Superposition is the value both}

A qubit in superposition is represented by the value $B$: truth and falsity are held together. This value is its own negation and is the one Belnap value that the classical slice cannot name.

The Hadamard gate sends either classical value to $B$ and sends $B$ back to a definite value. At the constructive layer, the step from a definite input to the held value is the engage operation. On the many-coordinate register, the same idea appears as a state spread across weight sectors. The uniform sector states are its symmetric instances.

\subsection{The Born rule is a forced quadratic projection}

The probability of an outcome is determined by the overlap between the state and one of the two poles. The state space used here is not assumed separately. It is the single-coordinate orbit derived in Proposition~\ref{prop:sphere}, namely the sphere $\mathbb{CP}^1$.

On that sphere, the true pole $T$ sits at $\theta=0$ and the false pole $F$ at $\theta=\pi$. The balanced held value $B$ lies on the equator. A pure state at polar angle $\theta$ is the ray $\ket{\psi}=\cos(\theta/2)\ket{T}+e^{i\varphi}\sin(\theta/2)\ket{F}$. The half-angle is forced by the spin one-half representation generated by the ladder.

A truth measurement uses the pair of antipodal projectors, giving
\[
p_T(\theta) = \bigl|\langle T\mid\psi\rangle\bigr|^2 = \cos^2\tfrac{\theta}{2}
= \frac{1+\cos\theta}{2}, \qquad p_F = \sin^2\tfrac{\theta}{2}, \qquad p_T+p_F=1,
\]
Thus $p_T=1$ at the true pole, $p_T=0$ at the false pole, and $p_T=1/2$ at the balanced value on the equator. The equatorial case recovers the cardinality reading $p_T = \mathrm{info}(\psi\cap\{T\})/\mathrm{info}(\psi)$.

The quadratic form is not added by hand. Busch's theorem~\cite{busch}, extending Gleason's theorem~\cite{gleason} to every dimension including two, states that an additive and non-contextual probability assignment on a positive-operator frame has the form $\mathrm{tr}(\rho E)$. For the truth projector on this sphere, that form is $\cos^2(\theta/2)$.

On the full carrier, $p_T+p_F$ falls below one by exactly the informational-layer weight that the truth measurement does not reach.

\subsection{The measurement frame is symmetric and informationally complete}

A measurement frame need not consist only of the two poles. The natural frame used here is a symmetric informationally complete positive-operator measurement. In $d$ dimensions it contains $d^2$ unit vectors with equal pairwise overlap.

For a qubit, $d=2$, so the frame has four vectors. They sit at the corners of a regular tetrahedron inscribed in the sphere. Any two distinct frame projectors $\Pi_i,\Pi_j$ satisfy
\[
\mathrm{tr}(\Pi_i\Pi_j) = \frac{1}{d+1} = \frac13,
\]
This is the equiangular condition. Because of it, the outcome probabilities determine the state completely, which is informational completeness. The frame does this with the fewest outcomes and with the most even possible spread, which is its symmetry.

On this frame the probability rule can be written in its frame-native form, the urgleichung of Fuchs and Schack. If a reference measurement has probabilities $p_i$ and a following measurement has probabilities $q_j$, then
\[
q_j = \sum_i\Bigl[(d+1)\,p_i - \frac1d\Bigr]\,r(j\mid i),
\]
Here $r(j\mid i)$ is the conditional probability of the second frame given the first. The subtraction of $1/d$ and the factor $d+1$ are the same coreflector coefficients that appear in the OPI matrix of Section 7. In this reading, those coefficients describe how the frame projects a paraconsistent value onto classical outcomes. The four corners of the tetrahedron are the four Belnap values placed symmetrically, and the equiangular overlap $1/(d+1)$ is the residue left by the held value in every direction at once.

\subsection{Interference is a winding on a lattice of turns}

The phase $e^{i\varphi}$ that produces interference is a winding, a rational number of turns, fixed at the commit operation. The native windings sit on a lattice of tenths.

\begin{proposition}[The gate lattice]\label{prop:tenths}
$\tfrac12=\tfrac{5}{10}$ lies on the tenths lattice, and $\tfrac18$ and $\tfrac14$ do not: $\tfrac18=\tfrac{p}{10}$ forces $p=5/4$ and $\tfrac14=\tfrac{p}{10}$ forces $p=5/2$, neither an integer.
\end{proposition}

Interference between two paths is the difference of their windings. A half turn lies on the native tenths lattice, while the quarter turn of $S$ and the eighth turn of $T$ do not. This lattice statement concerns the phases carried by the generators and does not by itself rule out exact realization by braid words, since products of noncommuting generators can have spectra not present among the generators themselves. In the Fibonacci representation the stronger word-level obstruction is arithmetic: the projective invariant $\operatorname{tr}^2/\det$ excludes $T$, while the determinant lattice supplies the additional obstruction needed to exclude $S$~\cite{gateobstruction}. By contrast, $Z$ and every relative tenth phase are realized exactly. Thus approximation of $S$ and $T$ is not an engineering accommodation to a finite gate dictionary. Their exact targets are absent from the braid image, so compilation is an arithmetic necessity. The fringe visibility is the information-order distance between the two path states.

\subsection{Entanglement is a persistent contradiction}

Two systems are entangled when they share a held value $B$ that persists as either side is read. The value survives refutation, is fixed by negation, and does not trivialize the logic. The resulting correlation therefore exceeds what can be represented by independent definite values.

The correlator is not supplied as an additional assumption. It is derived from the shared held value on the product of two single-coordinate registers.

The shared relation pairs $T$ on one part with $F$ on the other, but neither alternative is fixed on its own. The relation must also survive a common change of reading frame. That persistence is expressed by requiring the state's ray to remain invariant under the diagonal action $U\otimes U$ of Proposition~\ref{prop:sphere}.

The product of two spin one-half representations decomposes as $\tfrac12\otimes\tfrac12 = 0\oplus 1$. Within that product, the invariant ray is unique. It is the spin-zero state
\[
\ket{B_2} = \frac{\ket{TF}-\ket{FT}}{\sqrt2},
\]
Neither part is one-sided, and the joint value does not factor into independent values for the two parts. A common rotation changes neither the relation nor its readings. This state is the shared held value in the linear envelope.

\begin{proposition}[The correlator is a cosine]\label{prop:corr}
A Boolean reading of part one in direction $\mathbf{a}$ is the observable $A_{\mathbf a}=\mathbf{a}\cdot\boldsymbol{\sigma}$, and likewise $B_{\mathbf b}=\mathbf{b}\cdot\boldsymbol{\sigma}$ on part two. On the shared held value, $\langle B_2\mid \sigma_i\otimes\sigma_j\mid B_2\rangle = -\delta_{ij}$, so $E(\mathbf{a},\mathbf{b}) = \langle B_2\mid A_{\mathbf a}\otimes B_{\mathbf b}\mid B_2\rangle = -\,\mathbf{a}\cdot\mathbf{b}$, and for coplanar frames separated by $\Delta$, $E(\Delta) = -\cos\Delta$.
\end{proposition}

The natural invariant gives $-\cos\Delta$, since the held relation pairs $T$ against $F$. Reversing the Boolean label on one part, which is what pairing $T$ with $F$ invites, flips the sign to $E(\Delta)=+\cos\Delta$, the convention used elsewhere. Either way the cosine is the classical two-pole reading of the spin one-half geometry the ladder generates.

\begin{corollary}[The Tsirelson bound]\label{cor:tsirelson}
For four readings $A,A',B,B'$ built from unit vectors, each squares to the identity. The CHSH operator $\mathcal{C} = A\otimes(B+B') + A'\otimes(B-B')$ satisfies, up to the sign fixed by the CHSH convention, $\mathcal{C}^2 = 4I - [A,A']\otimes[B,B']$. Each dichotomic reading has operator norm one, so $\lVert[A,A']\rVert\leq 2$ and $\lVert[B,B']\rVert\leq 2$, giving $\lVert\mathcal{C}\rVert^2\leq 8$ and $\lVert\mathcal{C}\rVert\leq 2\sqrt2$. At relative tilts of $\pi/4$ the bound is attained~\cite{tsirelson,bell,chsh}.
\end{corollary}

The bound $2\sqrt2$ follows from the same spin one-half representation, with no quantum correlator supplied in advance. The shared held value supplies $\cos(\pi/4)$ per setting, and the four readings reach $4\cdot\tfrac{\sqrt2}{2}=2\sqrt2$.

