\subsection{Uncertainty and error correction are the composition rule}

A value produced inside a region and cleared by a reversal is lost unless an enclosing region banks a copy. Two quantities opened in regions that cannot nest cannot both be banked, so one is exposed when the other closes.

\begin{lemma}[Banking survives a reversal]\label{lem:bank}
A mark deposited, then held by a frame open across a reversal, then fused, is present after the fuse; a mark deposited with no enclosing frame open, then cleared by a reversal, is lost and no later fuse restores it.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
A deposit into an open frame is banked there; the reversal clears the register but not the open frame, and the fuse folds the frame's copy back by mark-wise union, restoring it. A deposit with no open frame is in the register only; the reversal removes it and no frame holds a copy.
\end{proof}

Uncertainty is the exposed case. Position and momentum are two counts whose holding regions cannot both enclose the other's computing region. One of the two is therefore always exposed. This gives $\Delta x\,\Delta p \geq c\hbar$, where $c$ is a positive order-one constant fixed by the rule. The value of $c$ is determined by the composition rule itself; it is the coefficient that appears when the two exposures are minimized subject to the nesting constraint. In the standard units, this coefficient is $1/2$, but its precise value is fixed by the geometry of the register rather than by an independent assumption.

Error correction is the deliberately banked case. An outer frame stays open while a reversal clears the working register, so the syndrome remains banked. The outer fuse then restores it, allowing the error to be corrected without disturbing the encoded value. The composition rule is the same in both cases. Arranged so that the value is banked, it is error correction. Forced by geometry to leave a value exposed, it is uncertainty.

\subsection{Decoherence empties the informational layer}

Decoherence removes the informational layer $\{t,f\}$ from the system by carrying it into the environment. To see the correspondence, write the one-qubit density matrix in the truth basis,
\[
\rho = \begin{pmatrix} p_T & c \\ \bar c & p_F \end{pmatrix},
\]
The diagonal entries $p_T,p_F$ are the populations. They are the truth and falsity support read by the sector projection. The off-diagonal term $c$ is the coherence, here identified with the informational-layer marks $t,f$ that carry weight without settling truth.

Coupling the system to an environment attenuates the off-diagonal term, $c\to c\,e^{-t/\tau}$, while leaving the diagonal unchanged. As $c\to0$, the state approaches its constructive shadow $\mathrm{diag}(p_T,p_F)$, which lies in the Belnap slice with an empty informational layer. At the balanced point, the held value $B$ has its entire content in the off-diagonal term, so it decays to the undetermined mixture.

Decoherence is therefore the emptying of $\{t,f\}$. The informational layer drains into the environment while the constructive layer remains. A large system read coarsely thus presents its constructive shadow rather than its held value, although that shadow is never the ground.

\subsection{Complementarity is the two swaps that factor negation}

Negation factors into two commuting swaps on disjoint pairs of marks: the truth swap exchanging $T$ with $F$, and the information swap exchanging $t$ with $f$. A measurement in the truth basis reads the pair moved by the truth swap; a measurement in the conjugate basis reads the pair moved by the information swap. Because the two swaps act on disjoint marks, fixing the value of one pair leaves the other pair free, so sharpening the constructive layer says nothing about the informational layer the conjugate measurement reads. Complementary observables are the two factors of the single negation, and their mutual unsharpness is the disjointness of the marks they move.

\subsection{The quantum eraser is the fuse}

Marking a path is a split that banks the which-path value in an open frame. While the frame remains open, the banked mark distinguishes the paths, so interference is absent.

Erasing the mark is the fuse that closes that frame. Because split banked the arms it opened, fuse recovers the held value and therefore recovers the interference. This is the banked invertibility of Section 5: the value is recovered from its image. Nothing is literally erased.

No restoration reaches into an earlier time. The round trip returns the state. Closing the frame simply returns the value previously opened by split, and the fringes that reappear were always available to the closed description. The quantum eraser is the reversibility of split and fuse read experimentally.

\subsection{Contextuality is the frame-dependence of the held value}

\begin{proposition}[No global classical assignment]\label{prop:context}
There is no assignment of a single classical value to a proposition in the state $B$ that agrees with every frame, since the truth frame filters $B$ to $T$ and the falsity frame filters $B$ to $F$, and $T\neq F$.
\end{proposition}

The impossibility is the inexpressibility of $B$ seen from the frames: the held value has no classical name, so its reading is whatever the frame filters it to, and different frames filter it differently. The value is one; its shadows are many, one per frame.

\subsection{Composite systems}

Two systems combine by taking the product of their carriers. An entangled state is a value of that product that cannot be factored into one value for each part.

A shared held relation has one part true and the other false, with the two alternatives held together as $B$ rather than settled separately. No separable pair of one-sided values can express that relation, because any such pair selects one alternative while the shared value holds both. The four Bell states are the four shared held values of the two-part product under the two swaps.

Reading one part is a split of the shared value. Because $B$ persists, that split does not destroy the shared value, and the correlation therefore survives however the two parts are read. Under products, the number of middle values multiplies. A many-part composite consequently grows quickly, which is the carrier's form of the exponential growth of state space with the number of parts.

\section{A century of experiments in FDE terms}

The experiments that built quantum mechanics are, read in order, a century of readings of the held value through classical frames. Each is stated here in the carrier's terms, the apparatus the frame and the result the frame's filtering of a value that was never one-sided.

\subsection{The double slit}

A particle sent through two slits lands in a fringe pattern that no assignment of a definite slit reproduces. In carrier terms the particle at the slits is the held value $B$, through-left and through-right held together, and the screen reads a truth measurement whose outcome probability is $\cos^2(\theta/2)$ in the winding difference of the two paths. Closing one slit fixes a one-sided value, $T$ or $F$, and the fringes go, since a fixed value has no held part to interfere. The pattern is the Born reading of Section 6.2 with the phase the path-winding of Section 6.3. Davisson and Germer's electron diffraction and the later single-electron buildup are the same reading with the particles arriving one at a time: each electron is the held value, and the pattern accretes because every one is filtered by the same frame.

\subsection{Stern-Gerlach}

A beam of atoms through an inhomogeneous field splits into two, not a continuum. The spin component along the field is a constructive-layer mark with two one-sided values, and the apparatus is the split opening the held value into its two arms $T$ and $F$. Sending one output through a second magnet at an angle is a fresh split on a fresh frame, and the reappearance of both outputs is the persistence of the held value under a change of frame, contextuality of Section 6.9 made mechanical.

\subsection{The photoelectric and Compton effects}

Light ejects electrons only above a frequency threshold, and scatters off them with a wavelength shift. Both fix the winding quantum of Section 6.3: energy is deposited in whole turns, the commit fixing one winding at a time, so below the threshold no single commit carries enough and above it each commit ejects one electron. Compton scattering is the same commit read as a two-body exchange, the winding shifted by the recoil. These are the irreversible-commit experiments, the one operation with no adjoint made visible.

\subsection{EPR and the Bell tests}

Einstein, Podolsky and Rosen argued that perfect correlation at a distance forces predetermined values. Bell's inequality and its tests answer that no assignment of predetermined one-sided values reproduces the correlations. In carrier terms the shared state is the persistent held value $B$ of Section 6.4, and the correlation reaches the Tsirelson bound $2\sqrt2$ because $B$ supplies $\cos(\pi/4)$ per setting where any one-sided assignment supplies at most the classical $2$. Aspect's experiments, and the loophole-free tests of 2015 that closed the locality and detection gaps at once, measure the held value surviving separation: reading one wing is a split that does not destroy the shared value, so the other wing's reading stays correlated however the settings are chosen.

\subsection{Wheeler's delayed choice and the quantum eraser}

Whether to insert the recombining element is chosen after the particle is past the slits, and the particle still shows fringes or not according to the late choice. This is the reversibility of the split and fuse of Section 6.7: the held value is carried intact until a frame is closed, and closing it late is the same fuse as closing it early, with no reach back in time because the round trip returns the banked value. Scully and Drühl's eraser and Kim's realization bank the which-path mark and then erase it, recovering the interference, the fuse restoring the value the split opened.

\subsection{Single-photon interference and Hong-Ou-Mandel}

Grangier's experiment shows a single photon interfering with itself while never being detected at two detectors at once: the held value spreads across both arms yet the commit deposits it in one place. Hong-Ou-Mandel's two-photon coincidence dip is the held value of a two-part system, the two photons entering the balanced held state under which the coincidence outcome cancels, the composite-system reading of Section 6.10 at two parts.

\subsection{Macroscopic held values}

Superconductivity, the Josephson effect, the SQUID, and Bose-Einstein condensation are held values sustained across a macroscopic number of parts. The condensate is the uniform sector state of Section 5.1 at large $m$, every part in the same held value, and the Josephson current is the winding difference of Section 6.3 read across a junction. The Leggett-Garg tests, which bound correlations across times rather than places, measure the held value persisting in one system through successive readings, the temporal form of the Bell argument, and their violation is the same persistence of $B$ under a change of frame.

\subsection{Tunneling and the vacuum shifts}

A particle crosses a barrier it classically cannot, the Lamb shift moves an atomic level, and the Casimir effect pulls two plates together. Each is the informational layer $\{t,f\}$ carrying weight where the constructive layer reads nothing: the barrier region holds a nonzero informational value the truth frame does not reach, so the commit can land beyond it, and the vacuum shifts are the informational layer's weight read as a force. The tunneling amplitude is the information-order distance across the barrier, positive where the truth-order reading is zero.

\subsection{Teleportation}

Quantum teleportation moves a held value from one place to another using a shared held value and two classical marks. In carrier terms the shared value is the persistent $B$ of Section 6.4, the two transmitted bits are the coreflector reading of which frame the sender's measurement filtered to, and the receiver's correction is the inverse frame that restores the value. Nothing one-sided is sent that carries the value; the value rides the held state, and the classical marks only name the frame. Teleportation is the coreflector and its inverse used as a channel.

\subsection{Aharonov-Bohm and neutron interferometry}

An electron kept out of a magnetic field still picks up a phase from the potential enclosed, and a neutron split across a height difference picks up a phase from gravity in the Colella-Overhauser-Werner experiment. Both are windings of Section 6.3 accumulated by the informational layer where the truth frame reads nothing: the field is zero on the path, so no constructive mark is deposited, yet the enclosed potential winds the phase, an informational-layer weight read as interference. The phase is the winding difference of the two arms, and it is present exactly where the classical force is absent.

\subsection{The quantum Zeno effect}

Repeatedly reading a system in the truth frame freezes it in the value last read. Each reading is a split that filters the held value to a pole, and a rapid sequence of splits never lets the value drift back to the held state between readings, so the value stays where the frame put it. Freezing by observation is the split applied faster than the held value can re-form, the reversibility of Section 5 outrun by the frame.

\subsection{Cavity quantum electrodynamics and photon counting}

Haroche's experiments count photons in a cavity without absorbing them, reading the field through atoms that cross it. Each atom is a nondemolition split: it reads one rung of the photon-number ladder of Section 5, the field's weight sector, while leaving the field to be read again, the fuse restoring the value the split opened. The whole sequence measures the ladder rung by rung without collapsing it, filtration made into an apparatus.

\subsection{Entanglement swapping and delayed-choice entanglement}

Two particles that never met become entangled when a joint reading is made on their partners, and the reading may be delayed past the particles' own detection. This is the persistence and reversibility of the held value together: the joint reading is a fuse that combines two shared held values into one, and because the fuse reaches back through no time the swap works whether the joint reading is early or late. The correlation appears between particles with no common past because the held value is carried rather than created by the reading.

\subsection{Large-molecule matter-wave interference}

Molecules of hundreds of atoms are sent through a grating and show fringes, the held value sustained across a large composite. This is the composite-system reading of Section 6.10 at large part count: the molecule's center of mass is the held value $B$, through-here and through-there together, and the fringe visibility is the information-order spread of the two paths. That the effect survives to large mass is the held value's indifference to part count, bounded only by the coupling that empties the informational layer, decoherence of Section 6.5.

\subsection{Weak measurement and Wigner's friend}

A weak reading extracts a little of the held value without fixing it, a partial split that leaves most of the value held, and the average of many weak readings recovers a value outside the one-sided range because it reads the held value rather than a pole. Wigner's friend, an observer inside a sealed laboratory read from outside, is two frames on one held value: the friend's frame filters it to a pole, the outside frame keeps it held, and the two disagree exactly as the truth and falsity frames disagree on $B$ in Proposition~\ref{prop:context}. The paradox is the frame-dependence of the held value with one frame nested inside the other.

\section{The tower of gauge ranks}

The four-valued carrier sits on the second rung of a larger tower. As the scale of a physical structure descends and its gauge rank increases, the number of independent one-sided marks increases with it.

At rank $n$, the carrier is the powerset of $2n$ marks. There are $n$ truth marks and $n$ falsity marks, paired with one another by negation. We write $\mathrm{FDE}(n)$ for this rank-$n$ carrier.

\begin{definition}[The tower]
$\mathrm{FDE}(n) = \mathcal{P}(\{T_1,\dots,T_n,F_1,\dots,F_n\})$ with negation exchanging $T_i$ with $F_i$, the information order the subset order, and the truth order the union of truth marks against the intersection of falsity marks as at rank one. $\mathrm{FDE}(2)$ is the four-valued carrier of this paper on the constructive layer.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}[The rungs realized]\label{prop:tower}
$\mathrm{FDE}(2)$ is the occupation of a single spatial orbital, its four Belnap values $N,T,F,B$ the empty, up, down, and paired occupations, negation the spin flip and the held value $B$ the doubly occupied orbital that carries both. $\mathrm{FDE}(3)$ is the three-valued colour charge of a quark, its truth marks the three colours and its falsity marks the three anticolours, negation colour conjugation. The limit $\mathrm{FDE}(\infty)$ is the carrier at unification, of unbounded rank.
\end{proposition}

The atomic rung is the occupation reading, not a spin reading: an electron has two internal spin states, but the four values are the empty, up, down, and paired occupations of the orbital it may sit in, and it is the paired occupation, both spins present in one orbital, that is the held value $B$. The exclusion that forbids two identical occupations is the constructive layer refusing a second copy of a one-sided mark, and the solidity of matter is that exclusion enforced across every orbital at once.

\begin{theorem}[Growth with rank, and loss on restriction]\label{thm:grow}
The number of truth values grows strictly and monotonically with the rank, and there is an embedding $\mathrm{FDE}(n)\hookrightarrow\mathrm{FDE}(n+1)$ with a restriction the other way whose composite with the embedding is the identity on $\mathrm{FDE}(n)$, while the restriction alone is not injective.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The carrier at rank $n$ has $2^{2n}$ values, strictly increasing in $n$. Embed $\mathrm{FDE}(n)$ into $\mathrm{FDE}(n+1)$ by carrying each mark to itself and leaving the new pair $T_{n+1},F_{n+1}$ empty; restrict back by deleting that pair. Restriction after embedding restores every value, since the embedded values never touch the new pair. But restriction alone identifies any two values differing only in the new pair, for example the value with $T_{n+1}$ present and the value with it absent both restrict to the same rank-$n$ value, so it is not injective.
\end{proof}

Restriction down the tower has the same structural form as the coreflector onto the Boolean core. It is a total map, it is a left inverse to the inclusion, and it is not injective. What it discards is exactly the pair of marks that the lower rung has no room to represent.

The passage from a finer scale to a coarser one therefore loses information in the same way as the passage from the paraconsistent ambient to the classical world. The worked instance in the next section measures one such loss numerically.

