\section{A worked realization: Optimal Polynomial Intersection}

We now turn to a complete worked instance. Here the classical image of a paraconsistent object can be computed directly, and its convergence toward the paraconsistent original can be measured. The object is the tridiagonal ladder of Section 5. The setting is a hard optimization problem whose quantum algorithm is governed by that ladder.

\subsection{The problem}

\begin{definition}[Optimal Polynomial Intersection]
Fix a prime $p$ and an integer $n<p-1$. Let $F_1,\dots,F_{p-1}$ be subsets of $\mathbb{F}_p$. An instance asks for a polynomial $Q\in\mathbb{F}_p[y]$ of degree at most $n-1$ maximizing
$f_{\mathrm{OPI}}(Q) = |\{y\in\{1,\dots,p-1\}: Q(y)\in F_y\}|$. It is balanced when every $|F_i|=r=\lfloor p/2\rfloor$.
\end{definition}

Optimal Polynomial Intersection can be read as a maximum-satisfaction problem over $\mathbb{F}_p$. The bounded-degree polynomials form the codewords of a linear code, so maximizing the intersection count is the same as maximizing the number of constraints satisfied by a codeword.

There are $m=p-1$ constraints. Their matrix is Vandermonde, and the dual code $C^{\perp}$ is Reed-Solomon. Berlekamp-Massey performs syndrome decoding of $C^{\perp}$ out to $\ell=\lfloor(n+1)/2\rfloor$ errors in polynomial time. The problem is a useful hard target because that decoder alone does not reach the optimum. The quantum algorithm improves on the classical baseline by concentrating amplitude on low-weight error patterns.

\subsection{The coding structure}

The satisfaction problem can be converted directly into a decoding problem. For each constraint $y$, choose a representative of $F_y$ and record a mismatch whenever $Q(y)\notin F_y$. The resulting mismatch vector has one bit per constraint. A polynomial's satisfaction count is therefore $m$ minus the Hamming weight of that vector.

The evaluations of all degree-$<n$ polynomials at the $m=p-1$ nonzero points form a Reed-Solomon code $C$ with a Vandermonde encoding. Its dual $C^{\perp}$ is also a Reed-Solomon code, with dimension $m-n$ and minimum distance $d^{\perp}=n+1$. Maximizing satisfaction is thus the same as finding the codeword of $C$ nearest the target in Hamming distance.

Berlekamp-Massey decodes the target syndrome with respect to $C^{\perp}$. It forms the syndrome polynomial, finds the shortest linear recurrence that generates it, and reads the mismatch positions from the roots of the error locator. It corrects up to $\ell=\lfloor(n+1)/2\rfloor = \lfloor d^{\perp}/2\rfloor$ errors in time cubic in the block length. This is the classical polynomial-time floor used here.

The same decoding radius $\ell$ bounds the dimension of the ladder matrix. The quantum state is supported on error patterns of weight at most $\ell$, with one weight sector per rung. The condition $2\ell+1<d^{\perp}$ in Lemma~\ref{lem:jordan} is exactly the unique-decodability condition. In that regime, each syndrome names one error pattern, and the ladder transfers amplitude only between adjacent weights.

\subsection{The algorithm and its matrix}

Decoded Quantum Interferometry~\cite{dqi} prepares a state whose amplitude is concentrated on low-weight error patterns and reads the decoded polynomial. Its expected performance is the largest eigenvalue of a finite matrix.

\begin{lemma}[The expected satisfied count]\label{lem:jordan}
Let $\langle s^{(m,\ell)}\rangle$ be the expected number of satisfied constraints after state preparation at decoding radius $\ell$. If $2\ell+1<d^{\perp}$, the minimum distance of $C^{\perp}$, then
\[
\langle s^{(m,\ell)}\rangle = \frac{mr}{p} + \frac{\sqrt{r(p-r)}}{p}\,
\lambda_{\max}\bigl(A^{(m,\ell,d)}\bigr),
\]
with $A^{(m,\ell,d)}$ the $(\ell+1)\times(\ell+1)$ real symmetric tridiagonal matrix, diagonal $kd$ for $k=0,\dots,\ell$, off-diagonal $a_{k+1}=\sqrt{(k+1)(m-k)}$, and $d=(p-2r)/\sqrt{r(p-r)}$.
\end{lemma}

This is the result of Jordan and colleagues~\cite{dqi}. Its matrix is exactly the filtration ladder from Theorem~\ref{thm:matrix}. The diagonal term $kd$ is the evaluate operator $D$, with $d=(p-2r)/\sqrt{r(p-r)}$. The off-diagonal term $\sqrt{(k+1)(m-k)}$ is the raising and lowering coefficient from Theorem~\ref{thm:ladder}. The expected satisfied count is therefore a constant plus the largest eigenvalue of the ladder, multiplied by the stated scale factor.

\begin{proposition}[The asymptotic limit]\label{prop:asymp}
Taking $\ell\to n/2$ and $m\to p$ gives
\[
\frac{\langle s\rangle_{\mathrm{DQI}}}{p} = \frac12 + \sqrt{\frac{n}{2p}\Bigl(1-\frac{n}{2p}\Bigr)},
\]
with no eigenvalue problem to solve.
\end{proposition}

The finite expression retains the full $(\ell+1)$-dimensional operator. It contains a specific finite $m$, a matrix, and an eigenvector. The asymptotic expression is what remains after that structure is smoothed into a single scalar as $m\to p$.

At every finite $m$, the finite form therefore carries strictly more structure than the scalar limit. Moving from the finite operator to the asymptotic scalar is a coarsening, in the same direction as the coreflector of Theorem~\ref{thm:two}.

\subsection{The paraconsistent original}

We claim the matrix $A^{(m,\ell,d)}$ is the classical image of a paraconsistent object, and the algorithm's expectation value is that object read through the coreflector. We exhibit the object, the lift, and the identification.

The syndrome-decoding structure of the algorithm can be represented as a closing program over the Belnap slice. Its relevant structural feature is an outer frame that banks a count across an uncomputation step.

An automatically designed thirty-three-operation program for this structure did not close. The closure audit located the failure precisely. A count was being cleared before the frame that should have banked it had opened. In the language of Lemma~\ref{lem:bank}, the result was exposed instead of banked. The same diagnosis appeared at every rotation of the program's orbit and at the same structural location: reversal cleared the count before its holding frame existed.

The composition rule also supplies the repair. Open the holding frame before the computing frame. Implementing that change adds five operations, three splits and two fuses, producing a thirty-eight-operation program that closes. Its banked count survives every reversal. The cyclic verdict closes at every rotation of the orbit, and an exhaustive audit finds the repaired program holding the value at every cut where the original program exposed it.

This gives a constructed and mechanically verified paraconsistent program of the class whose classical image is the algorithm's circuit.

\begin{proposition}[The $m$-fold lift]\label{prop:lift}
Let $\iota$ and the coreflector $c$ act coordinatewise on $\{N,T,F,B\}^m$, ordered coordinatewise in the truth order. Then the coordinatewise inclusion is left adjoint to the coordinatewise coreflector, and the Boolean image $\mathbf{B}^m\cong\{F,T\}^m$ is the algorithm's classical register $\mathbb{F}_2^m$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
For $u\in\mathbf{B}^m$ and $v\in\{N,T,F,B\}^m$, the coordinatewise order gives $\iota^m(u)\leqt v$ iff $\iota(u_i)\leqt v_i$ for all $i$, and $u\leqt c^m(v)$ iff $u_i\leqt c(v_i)$ for all $i$; by Theorem~\ref{thm:two} each coordinate satisfies $\iota(u_i)\leqt v_i \iff u_i\leqt c(v_i)$, so the two coincide. The Boolean image $\{F,T\}^m$ is $\mathbb{F}_2^m$ under $F\mapsto0$, $T\mapsto1$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[The matrix is the coreflector image of the paraconsistent ladder]\label{thm:main}
On the $m$-fold paraconsistent register, with $\ket{k}$ the uniform superposition of states with $k$ coordinates at $T$, the split operation is the raising operator $S_{+}$, the fuse operation its adjoint $S_{-}$, and the evaluate operation the diagonal $D$ reading $d$ once per coordinate at $T$. Then $S_{-}\ket{k}=\sqrt{k(m-k+1)}\ket{k-1}$ and $D\ket{k}=kd\ket{k}$ are the off-diagonal $a_k$ and the diagonal $kd$ of $A^{(m,\ell,d)}$ in Lemma~\ref{lem:jordan}, term for term, at $d=(p-2r)/\sqrt{r(p-r)}$. Read through the coreflector of Theorem~\ref{thm:two}, $A^{(m,\ell,d)}$ is the effective operator governing the algorithm's expectation value $\langle s^{(m,\ell)}\rangle$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The three operations realize as the three operators by the definitions of Section 5: opening a frame raises the weight, closing lowers it, depositing reads the present weight. Their matrix elements on the uniform sector states are those of Theorem~\ref{thm:ladder} and Proposition~\ref{prop:diag}, which are the entries of $A^{(m,\ell,d)}$ in Lemma~\ref{lem:jordan}. The coreflector $c^m$ of Proposition~\ref{prop:lift} maps the paraconsistent register onto the Boolean register $\mathbb{F}_2^m$ on which the algorithm reads its expectation value, and Lemma~\ref{lem:jordan} expresses that value through $\lambda_{\max}(A^{(m,\ell,d)})$, so the classical expectation value is the largest eigenvalue of the paraconsistent ladder read through the coreflector.
\end{proof}

The matrix governing the quantum algorithm is therefore the ladder of a paraconsistent object after that ladder has been read down to the classical register by the coreflector. The algorithm's performance is consequently a reading of the paraconsistent object through the lossy retraction of Theorem~\ref{thm:lossy}.

\subsection{Direct computation of the classical image}

Both columns of the convergence table below come from one computation, not two sources. Three procedures run every row.

The finite eigenvalue is the shifted power iteration. Build the $(\ell+1)\times(\ell+1)$ tridiagonal $A^{(m,\ell,d)}$ of Lemma~\ref{lem:jordan}. Power iteration on $A$ converges to the eigenvalue of largest magnitude, which need not be the largest eigenvalue when the spectrum straddles zero. Shift by a bound on the spectral radius, iterating on $A + \sigma I$ with $\sigma = \|A\|_\infty$ so that $A+\sigma I$ is positive semidefinite:

\begin{enumerate}
\item set $v$ to a random unit vector of length $\ell+1$;
\item repeat until the Rayleigh quotient settles: $w \leftarrow (A+\sigma I)v$, then $v\leftarrow w/\|w\|$;
\item return $\lambda_{\max}(A) = v^{\top}(A+\sigma I)v - \sigma$.
\end{enumerate}

Each matrix-vector product is linear in $\ell$ because $A$ is tridiagonal, so the whole solve is linear in the ladder dimension and reaches the $2478\times2478$ ladder in seconds. Substituting $\lambda_{\max}$ into Lemma~\ref{lem:jordan} gives the exact column.

The asymptotic scalar is computed in the same run from the same $p$ and $n$ by the closed form of Proposition~\ref{prop:asymp}, so the asymptotic column is output rather than a value copied in by hand.

The classical decoder baseline is Prange's algorithm, run for real:

\begin{enumerate}
\item draw a random size-$n$ subset $S$ of the constraints;
\item interpolate the unique degree-$<n$ polynomial $Q$ agreeing with one representative of $F_y$ at each $y\in S$;
\item score $Q$ by its satisfaction count against all $m$ constraints;
\item repeat over many draws and keep the best score.
\end{enumerate}

The finite eigenvalue, the asymptotic scalar, and the decoder baseline are all executed for every prime in the table.

