$10007$ & $2478\times2478$ & $0.9296$ & $0.9316$ \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

Across the four scales, the exact value increases monotonically. With each doubling of $p$, it closes roughly one third of the gap that still separates it from the asymptotic value.

At finite $m$, the description is operator-valued. As $m$ grows, that richer description approaches the poorer scalar limit. The direction is the same one taken by the coreflector of Theorem~\ref{thm:two}, but here the approach is measured rather than assumed.

Theorem~\ref{thm:main} identifies the finite matrix as the classical reading of the paraconsistent ladder. There is therefore no second, independently quantum object beside the matrix in this construction. There is the paraconsistent ladder and its classical image. The finite-size convergence makes the coarsening of Theorem~\ref{thm:lossy} quantitative: the operator-valued original stands above the scalar image and approaches it as the retraction is approached.

The ladder's spectrum explains the shape of this convergence. The off-diagonal entries of $A^{(m,\ell,d)}$ are $\sqrt{(k+1)(m-k)}$, which peak near the middle rung. The resulting eigenvalue density is a semicircle centered on the diagonal drift, with a half-width set by the off-diagonal scale $\sqrt{m}$.

The largest eigenvalue lies at the upper edge of that semicircle. As $m$ grows, the smooth semicircle law used by the asymptotic expression in Proposition~\ref{prop:asymp} describes that edge increasingly well. At finite $m$, however, the matrix has a discrete edge below the smooth one.

The gap between the discrete and smooth edges is the finite-size correction. At finite $m$ there are only $\ell+1$ rungs, so the top eigenvector cannot spread as freely as the continuum permits. As the number of rungs increases, the finite value rises toward the limit. The operator-valued original is precisely the discrete structure that the scalar limit smooths away.

The practical ceiling on pushing the table to larger $p$ is not the eigenvalue solve, which is linear in the ladder dimension and reaches the largest listed size in seconds regardless of $m$, but the interpolation step of the classical decoder, cubic in the polynomial degree. That is a limit on the baseline comparison, not on the quantity the convergence is about, and it is the natural next point to lift with a shared-denominator reformulation of the interpolation.

\section{Theorems as measurements}

The worked instance measures one object, the OPI ladder, through the coreflector. The frame used for that reading is one cell in a larger structure. The wider program treats other hard problems as other cells of the same operator-valued measure.

In the general sense used here, a measurement is a positive-operator-valued measure. It is a family of positive operators that sum to the identity, with each operator assigning an outcome probability through $\mathrm{tr}(\rho E_i)$.

The symmetric informationally complete frame of Section 6.3 is the maximally even form of such a measure. In this reading, the grammar's twelve operations are its outcomes, meaning the distinctions that a frame can draw.

Positivity in this frame is a property of winding. A measurement element is positive exactly when its winding is self-inverse, a half-turn that squares to the identity. The positive cells are therefore the self-inverse windings. The indefinite cells are the windings that do not close on themselves in one step.

Under this interpretation, the hard open problems addressed by the program occupy different cells of one operator-valued-measure taxonomy. At each address, a paraconsistent object is read through a frame.

The taxonomy has a fixed twenty-four-cell structure. The self-inverse condition selects the positive cells. Each conjecture occupies one cell: the object is paraconsistent, the frame is classical, and the conjecture states that the resulting classical reading has a particular value.

The OPI instance of Section 7 is the worked case in which that reading is computed and its convergence toward the paraconsistent original is measured. The other problems are the same construction at other addresses. On this account, proving one of them means widening the frame until the paraconsistent original can be read without loss. This is the Naimark dilation that lifts the operator-valued measure to a projective measurement on a larger space.

The program's claim is therefore that the hard problems are one object appearing at many addresses, with the worked instance providing the first complete reading.

\section{The arrow of time and the cost of measurement}

The reversibility of the split and fuse has a thermodynamic reading. Landauer's principle sets a floor on the energy any logically irreversible step must dissipate: erasing one bit of information costs at least $k_B T\ln 2$, because erasure maps two states to one and the lost distinction leaves as heat. Bennett showed that a computation built entirely from reversible steps owes no such cost, since nothing is erased, and Fredkin and Toffoli gave a universal reversible logic that carries this out in principle at no dissipation floor.

Measurement as filtration is reversible. The split opens a value into tensor factors, a reading selects one, and the fuse restores the whole, so no factor is erased and the ambient pays no Landauer cost for a measurement. What a collapse account records as the destruction of the unread outcomes is, in the ladder, the classical image keeping one rung and dropping the others, and it is only that image, the coreflector's reading, where an erasure and its cost appear. The dissipation of a real measuring device is the cost of maintaining the classical record, not of the filtration itself.

The arrow of time is the accumulation of fixes. The one operation with no adjoint is the commit, and every commit records a value irreversibly into the winding, a step the fuse cannot undo. A computation of only splits and fuses runs equally well in either direction; a computation that fixes has a direction, the direction in which the fixes accumulate. The thermodynamic arrow, the growth of the classical record, is the count of commits, and the reversibility of the rest is why the ambient itself carries no arrow while its classical image does.

Approaching the Landauer floor in practice requires the whole computational stack to be aligned. The computation itself must be reversible, with its operations closing so that every step is invertible and its result recoverable. The operating system must also stop erasing, reclaiming transient state by scope teardown rather than abandoning it. The hardware must then implement reversible gates instead of the dissipative gates used by ordinary processors.

When all three levels are in syzygy, the Landauer floor is approached. If any one level erases, the floor is not reached, and the remaining gap is the erasure forced by that stage. A reversible computation run on an ordinary irreversible stack therefore shows a positive marginal draw above idle. In this account, that draw is the tax of the erasing stack rather than a cost owed by the reversible computation itself.

\section{Summary of results}

The carrier is the sixteen-value trilattice with three orders, each of which is a lattice (Proposition~\ref{prop:orders}). Negation is an involution fixing exactly the two nonclassical values $N$ and $B$ (Proposition~\ref{prop:neg}). The consequence relation neither explodes on a contradiction nor validates excluded middle (Theorem~\ref{thm:entail}).

The Boolean core is reached by two distinct truth-order adjunctions, ex falso and ex quodlibet (Theorem~\ref{thm:two}), and neither is an information-order adjunction (Proposition~\ref{prop:noinfo}). The collapse is exactly lossy because the held value is inexpressible in either image (Theorem~\ref{thm:lossy}).

On an $m$-fold register, split and fuse are the raising and lowering operators on the weight sectors, with $S_+\ket{k}=\sqrt{(k+1)(m-k)}\ket{k+1}$ and its adjoint (Theorem~\ref{thm:ladder}). Evaluate is the diagonal $D\ket{k}=kd\ket{k}$ (Proposition~\ref{prop:diag}). Together they form the symmetric tridiagonal filtration matrix (Theorem~\ref{thm:matrix}), and measurement is filtration through this ladder rather than collapse.

The register is the canonical linearization $\ell^2(\{T,F\}^m)$ of the carrier (Definition~\ref{def:lin}). Its ladder generates the su(2) action of spin $m/2$ (Proposition~\ref{prop:su2}), and the single-coordinate orbit is the qubit sphere $\mathbb{CP}^1$ (Proposition~\ref{prop:sphere}). The Hilbert-space geometry is therefore derived from the ladder rather than assumed.

On that derived sphere, the Born rule is the forced quadratic $\cos^2(\theta/2)$ (Section 6.2, via Busch~\cite{busch}). The native gate windings sit on a lattice of tenths (Proposition~\ref{prop:tenths}), and in the Fibonacci representation the corresponding word-level arithmetic obstruction separates exact native phases from gates that can only be approached: the tenth phases are exact, while $S$ and $T$ remain outside the braid image at every word length~\cite{gateobstruction}. The shared held value is the unique frame-invariant ray, giving the correlator $E(\Delta)=-\cos\Delta$ (Proposition~\ref{prop:corr}) and the Tsirelson bound $2\sqrt2$ (Corollary~\ref{cor:tsirelson}). Uncertainty and error correction are two readings of the same composition rule (Lemma~\ref{lem:bank}), and there is no global classical assignment to a held value (Proposition~\ref{prop:context}).

The tower of gauge ranks grows the number of values strictly with rank. Its restriction map is a non-injective left inverse of the embedding (Theorem~\ref{thm:grow}), which is the same structural form as the classical coreflector.

Finally, the DQI matrix for Optimal Polynomial Intersection is the coreflector image of a paraconsistent ladder, entry for entry (Theorem~\ref{thm:main}). Across four scales, the finite operator-valued value settles monotonically onto the scalar limit in the direction of the coreflector (Section 9).

\section{Discussion}

One object has been traced through three descriptions. As a paraconsistent object it is the split, fuse, and evaluate ladder on the $m$-fold register, an operator-valued object that closes under the composition rule. As a classical image it is the tridiagonal matrix $A^{(m,\ell,d)}$, the coreflector's reading of that ladder on the Boolean register, governing the quantum algorithm's expectation value. As an asymptotic limit it is the single scalar of Proposition~\ref{prop:asymp}, what the matrix becomes when its structure is smoothed as $m\to p$. The three are one object at three levels of coarsening, each arrow proved or measured: the coreflector from the paraconsistent to the classical, the limit from the finite matrix to the scalar.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\resizebox{\linewidth}{!}{%
\begin{tikzpicture}[vox, node distance=24mm]
  \node[voxbox, minimum width=34mm] (word)
    {paraconsistent ladder\\split, evaluate, fuse\\$S_+ + D + S_-$};

  \node[voxbox, right=of word, minimum width=50mm] (matrix)
    {$A(m,\ell,d)$\\[-1mm]
    $\begin{bmatrix}
      0 & \sqrt m & & \\
      \sqrt m & d & \sqrt{2(m-1)} & \\
      & \ddots & \ddots & \ddots \\
      & & \sqrt{\ell(m-\ell+1)} & \ell d
    \end{bmatrix}$};

  \node[voxbox, right=of matrix, minimum width=31mm] (eig)
    {finite reading\\$\lambda_{\max}(A)$};

  \node[voxbox, right=of eig, minimum width=30mm] (lim)
    {asymptotic image\\$\lambda_\infty$};

  \draw[voxarrow] (word) -- node[voxlabel,above] {coreflector} (matrix);
  \draw[voxarrow] (matrix) -- node[voxlabel,above] {spectral read} (eig);
  \draw[voxarrow] (eig) -- node[voxlabel,above] {$m\to\infty$} (lim);

  \node[below=6mm of word, font=\scriptsize] {operator-valued original};
  \node[below=6mm of matrix, font=\scriptsize] {finite classical image};
  \node[below=6mm of lim, font=\scriptsize] {scalar coarsening};
\end{tikzpicture}}
\caption{The worked DQI/OPI realization as successive coarsenings of one object: paraconsistent ladder, finite tridiagonal matrix, spectral value, and asymptotic scalar.}
\label{fig:dqi-coarsening}
\end{figure}

This is the program's thesis in one worked case. The quantum algorithm is the classical image of a paraconsistent object, and the image is strictly poorer than the original in a way that can be measured. The apparent quantum behavior, the concentration of amplitude that gives the algorithm its advantage, is produced by the classical evaluation of the paraconsistent ladder, with no additional physical ingredient. The relationship is directional: the matrix is derived from the ladder, the scalar from the matrix, and neither lower description asserts anything back up.

The tensor filtration of Section 5 is the mechanism throughout. The split and fuse are the raising and lowering operators, the evaluate is the diagonal, and the whole filtration is the tridiagonal ladder they build. Measurement is filtration through that ladder, selecting a rung while the ladder persists, and the reversibility of the split and fuse is why there is no collapse and no thermodynamic floor forcing dissipation. The worked instance shows the same ladder governing a real algorithm, its largest eigenvalue the algorithm's performance, its finite-to-asymptotic collapse the coreflector's coarsening measured across four scales.

Look around you. The definite classical world of one-sided facts is the special configuration that does not occur; a genuinely classical object would not hold together, have color, or resist collapse, because its stability is the exclusion of paired occupations, the value $B$ across every atom. What occurs is the ambient. The classical image is what a retraction reads off it and the asymptotic scalar is what a limit reads off the image, and at no level is the classical description the ground. It is a coarsening, the coarsening is exactly lossy, and the loss is the state that carries contradiction.

The ambient holds both, and in holding both, it holds everything together. The logic is prior to the physics. The physics is the image of the logic under a retraction that cannot name the middle. And the middle, the held value, is the only thing that makes the world the strange, coherent, contradictory, persistent place that it is.

